WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:03.736 line:0%
JOY: Oh, my goodness!
You are crazy!

00:00:03.736 --> 00:00:06.706 line:0%
(horse neighs)

00:00:06.706 --> 00:00:09.142 line:0%
ZACK: Argh!

00:00:09.142 --> 00:00:10.810 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Next on</i>
 Secrets of the Zoo...

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:12.912 line:0%
SUZI: Oooh! Oooh!

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:15.348 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Doctors rush
 to save a cheetah...</i>

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:16.148 line:0%
SUZI: Ready?

00:00:16.148 --> 00:00:17.384 line:0%
PRIYA: Easy, my love.

00:00:18.984 --> 00:00:21.354 line:0%
SUZI: You're my baby boy.

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:24.557 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Rescued macaws get
 a new life at the zoo.</i>

00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:25.991 line:0%
(bird moaning)
DAVID: There she is.

00:00:25.991 --> 00:00:27.360 line:0%
SUZI: There's your
new bird, David.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.160 line:0%
DAVID: Yeah.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:29.128 line:0%
CARRIE: Ooooh!

00:00:29.128 --> 00:00:32.165 line:0%
DEVON: Good job! Yay!

00:00:32.165 --> 00:00:35.502 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: And polar bear cubs
 go off the deep end.</i>

00:00:37.803 --> 00:00:39.405 line:0%
<i> 10,000 animals.</i>

00:00:39.405 --> 00:00:41.240 line:0%
<i> Two locations.</i>

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:43.410 line:0%
<i> 150 caregivers.</i>

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:44.443 line:0%
(roar)

00:00:44.443 --> 00:00:47.147 line:0%
<i> This is</i> Secrets of the Zoo.

00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:52.385 line:0%
EMILY: Here she comes!
Incoming! Woo!

00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:56.122 line:0%
So we have two of our
three baby cheetahs

00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:57.457 line:0%
here in the watering hole.

00:00:57.457 --> 00:00:58.757 line:0%
These girls are four months old.

00:00:58.757 --> 00:01:00.192 line:0%
We get them out here
as often as possible

00:01:00.192 --> 00:01:03.830 line:0%
just to get those muscles
ready to run.

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:05.398 line:0%
So we have our yellow Labs,

00:01:05.398 --> 00:01:07.333 line:0%
we raise our Labs
with the cheetahs.

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:09.502 line:0%
They're kind of like
a security blanket, I would say.

00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:13.105 line:0%
It's a sibling that
has more confidence.

00:01:13.105 --> 00:01:15.341 line:0%
So if one of our cats is
just a little bit nervous,

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:18.511 line:0%
the dog kind of walks first
and that cat will follow him.

00:01:18.511 --> 00:01:20.079 line:0%
And they love running
maybe a little bit more

00:01:20.079 --> 00:01:23.249 line:0%
than the cheetahs do.

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:24.350 line:0%
(dog barking)

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:25.952 line:0%
SUZI: Perfect.

00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:28.421 line:0%
That's perfect. That's perfect.

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:30.556 line:0%
Are you a good boy?

00:01:30.556 --> 00:01:31.957 line:0%
PRIYA: Are you ready
to bring him?

00:01:31.957 --> 00:01:32.959 line:0%
SUZI: It's alright, buddy.

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:35.695 line:0%
PRIYA: We have two minutes.

00:01:35.695 --> 00:01:38.431 line:0%
Yes, two and a half minutes
to leave.

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:40.433 line:0%
SUZI: Come on, Emmett.
We need to go.

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:41.734 line:0%
You're stalling.

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:42.568 line:0%
He knows.

00:01:42.568 --> 00:01:43.869 line:0%
Come on, come on!

00:01:43.869 --> 00:01:45.371 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Emmett,
 a nine-month-old cheetah</i>

00:01:45.371 --> 00:01:48.775 line:0%
<i> is a fighter, overcoming
 the odds since a baby,</i>

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:50.843 line:0%
<i> hand-raised by the zoo staff.</i>

00:01:50.843 --> 00:01:53.513 line:0%
SUZI: Emmett was a very
compromised cat when we got him.

00:01:53.513 --> 00:01:56.248 line:0%
He was about two weeks old,
he was full of pneumonia,

00:01:56.248 --> 00:02:00.053 line:0%
Bordetella, feline herpes;
he was just about dead.

00:02:00.053 --> 00:02:02.688 line:0%
None of the other cats
survived in the litter.

00:02:02.688 --> 00:02:04.490 line:0%
Here's your buddies!

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:07.293 line:0%
Oh, they're all here!

00:02:07.293 --> 00:02:08.294 line:0%
(laughs)

00:02:08.294 --> 00:02:10.897 line:0%
You guys, wish him luck.

00:02:10.897 --> 00:02:13.466 line:0%
But unfortunately,
as he grew bigger,

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:16.536 line:0%
we noticed his shoulder
never fully developed.

00:02:16.536 --> 00:02:19.438 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Emmett's joint is
 missing bone and cartilage.</i>

00:02:19.438 --> 00:02:21.674 line:0%
<i> Untreated, Emmett's shoulder
 may restrict him</i>

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:24.143 line:0%
<i> to a painful life
 without running.</i>

00:02:24.143 --> 00:02:25.678 line:0%
SUZI: Are you going with him?

00:02:25.678 --> 00:02:27.580 line:0%
Are you going to be
his little buddy?

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.015 line:0%
You are his rock.

00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:30.149 line:0%
You're not going.

00:02:30.149 --> 00:02:32.051 line:0%
You have to stay, I know.

00:02:32.051 --> 00:02:33.686 line:0%
So we're on our way
to Ohio State,

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:36.022 line:0%
and he will have surgery.

00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:40.325 line:0%
PRIYA: Alright, let's go!

00:02:40.325 --> 00:02:41.728 line:0%
SUZI: I have had
two cheetahs here

00:02:41.728 --> 00:02:43.662 line:0%
that I put my heart and soul in,

00:02:43.662 --> 00:02:45.564 line:0%
and he's one of them.

00:02:45.564 --> 00:02:46.765 line:0%
It's nerve-racking.

00:02:46.765 --> 00:02:49.735 line:0%
I mean, he's got a big part
of my heart.

00:02:49.735 --> 00:02:53.639 line:0%
(sighs) Valium for me.

00:02:53.639 --> 00:02:55.040 line:0%
PRIYA: I have six bottles.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:57.210 line:0%
So you're in good shape.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:58.711 line:0%
SUZI: Thank you.

00:03:01.046 --> 00:03:04.584 line:0%
(music)

00:03:04.584 --> 00:03:07.086 line:0%
(squawking)

00:03:07.086 --> 00:03:09.421 line:0%
JOY: Hi, Kitty!
How are you, darling?

00:03:09.421 --> 00:03:11.090 line:0%
So that's my daughter.
(squawking)

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:13.025 line:0%
And you can hear her calling me.

00:03:13.025 --> 00:03:14.026 line:0%
What are you doing?

00:03:14.026 --> 00:03:15.428 line:0%
Hi!

00:03:15.428 --> 00:03:16.395 line:0%
Yeah, I know.

00:03:16.395 --> 00:03:18.864 line:0%
I'm gonna come see you.

00:03:18.864 --> 00:03:21.967 line:0%
I have a flamingo daughter
named Haleakala.

00:03:21.967 --> 00:03:23.069 line:0%
And she likes to greet me

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:25.871 line:0%
every time I go into
the flamingo yard.

00:03:25.871 --> 00:03:27.207 line:0%
Haleakala!

00:03:27.207 --> 00:03:29.542 line:0%
Where's that bird?
Where's that girl?

00:03:29.542 --> 00:03:30.609 line:0%
Come here, little lady!

00:03:30.609 --> 00:03:32.812 line:0%
I want a hug.

00:03:32.812 --> 00:03:36.983 line:0%
She in particular will yell
at me and then charge on over.

00:03:36.983 --> 00:03:40.686 line:0%
Oh, my goodness! You're crazy.

00:03:40.686 --> 00:03:43.656 line:0%
And she runs right into me
and then does a happy dance.

00:03:43.656 --> 00:03:45.991 line:0%
Are you happy I came to see you?

00:03:45.991 --> 00:03:47.360 line:0%
Yay!

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:49.428 line:0%
(squawking)

00:03:49.428 --> 00:03:51.397 line:0%
She pulls at my heartstrings.

00:03:51.397 --> 00:03:54.366 line:0%
So that's not a relationship
that happens every day,

00:03:54.366 --> 00:03:57.237 line:0%
and we really cherish the ones
that we do have like that.

00:03:59.472 --> 00:04:02.408 line:0%
Our relationship started back
when she was in the egg.

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:04.310 line:0%
We help raise our flamingos,

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:07.246 line:0%
and they hear our voice
in the egg as they're hatching,

00:04:07.246 --> 00:04:10.950 line:0%
and sometimes the flamingos will
imprint on particular people.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.719 line:0%
You never stop talking.
(squawking)

00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:14.721 line:0%
You never stop talking, do you?

00:04:14.721 --> 00:04:18.624 line:0%
And this one in particular has
a personality or flamingo-ality

00:04:18.624 --> 00:04:20.159 line:0%
that is very rambunctious.

00:04:20.159 --> 00:04:22.028 line:0%
(squawking) Thank you, buddy.

00:04:22.028 --> 00:04:23.896 line:0%
There's a flamingo hairstyle.

00:04:23.896 --> 00:04:26.832 line:0%
They might be hairdressers if
they had professions as humans.

00:04:26.832 --> 00:04:28.133 line:0%
It's not always
the best hairstyle

00:04:28.133 --> 00:04:31.170 line:0%
that you get at the end,
but they seem to enjoy it.

00:04:33.373 --> 00:04:36.776 line:0%
Oof, ha ha, alright,
let me see your feet.

00:04:36.776 --> 00:04:39.411 line:0%
Several of them
do have leg tapings.

00:04:39.411 --> 00:04:41.914 line:0%
And this is because
their growth plates

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:44.717 line:0%
when they're growing
are sometimes not even.

00:04:44.717 --> 00:04:47.587 line:0%
In the wild, they would be less
likely to survive a long life.

00:04:47.587 --> 00:04:48.988 line:0%
Check those wings.

00:04:48.988 --> 00:04:50.823 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Haleakala will be
 heading to the pool</i>

00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:52.891 line:0%
<i> to begin hydrotherapy sessions,</i>

00:04:52.891 --> 00:04:55.227 line:0%
<i> so her leg condition
 doesn't worsen.</i>

00:04:55.227 --> 00:04:57.263 line:0%
JOY: We're working with them
getting them up and moving,

00:04:57.263 --> 00:04:59.765 line:0%
and one of the best ways
is hydrotherapy,

00:04:59.765 --> 00:05:01.400 line:0%
or just moving around in water

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:04.437 line:0%
and kind of stimulate the body
to correct itself.

00:05:04.437 --> 00:05:05.771 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR:
 Joy will prep the pool</i>

00:05:05.771 --> 00:05:09.274 line:0%
<i> for Haleakala's hydrotherapy
 later today.</i>

00:05:09.274 --> 00:05:11.811 line:0%
JOY: I got to go.
You got to be a bird, okay?

00:05:11.811 --> 00:05:13.579 line:0%
I'm going to see you later.

00:05:13.579 --> 00:05:14.680 line:0%
Love you!

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:18.117 line:0%
Okay, stay, stay, okay.
(squawking)

00:05:18.117 --> 00:05:20.686 line:0%
I got to go. I got to go.
(squawking)

00:05:20.686 --> 00:05:22.355 line:0%
I'm sorry!

00:05:24.323 --> 00:05:27.159 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: 16 macaws rescued
 from a hoarding situation</i>

00:05:27.159 --> 00:05:31.097 line:0%
<i> in Alabama will soon arrive
 at the Columbus Zoo.</i>

00:05:32.798 --> 00:05:34.767 line:0%
WOUTER: Oh, my goodness.
HEATHER: Oh! There they are!

00:05:34.767 --> 00:05:38.070 line:0%
EMILY: The exotic pet market
is not great.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:40.639 line:0%
Macaws can go for
thousands of dollars,

00:05:40.639 --> 00:05:44.243 line:0%
and so this guy was probably
breeding them to sell.

00:05:44.243 --> 00:05:46.845 line:0%
They were living in caging
with no doors,

00:05:46.845 --> 00:05:48.814 line:0%
with moldy water filters.

00:05:48.814 --> 00:05:50.750 line:0%
They were getting
wet dog food to eat.

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:51.917 line:0%
It's just a horrible situation,

00:05:51.917 --> 00:05:53.652 line:0%
you know, you hate
to hear about that,

00:05:53.652 --> 00:05:56.922 line:0%
but hopefully their story has a
happy ending with us at the zoo.

00:05:56.922 --> 00:05:59.592 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The macaws are lucky
 to be alive.</i>

00:05:59.592 --> 00:06:01.561 line:0%
<i> Now the team will determine
 if they're well enough</i>

00:06:01.561 --> 00:06:04.296 line:0%
<i> to survive the drive
 back to Ohio.</i>

00:06:04.296 --> 00:06:05.865 line:0%
EMILY: Birdies!
WOUTER: Look at them!

00:06:05.865 --> 00:06:07.566 line:0%
HEATHER: Hi!
EMILY: You're cute.

00:06:07.566 --> 00:06:08.700 line:0%
HEATHER: They look
so much better

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:09.801 line:0%
than I thought they
were going to look.

00:06:09.801 --> 00:06:11.103 line:0%
EMILY: Oh, yeah,
they look great.

00:06:11.103 --> 00:06:12.638 line:0%
You're coming home!

00:06:12.638 --> 00:06:14.339 line:0%
Are you coming to the zoo?

00:06:14.339 --> 00:06:16.409 line:0%
(chirps)
Yes!

00:06:16.409 --> 00:06:17.310 line:0%
HEATHER: You already
trained him.

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:18.745 line:0%
EMILY: Yes.

00:06:18.745 --> 00:06:19.945 line:0%
HEATHER: We weren't expecting
these birds to be socialized

00:06:19.945 --> 00:06:21.581 line:0%
to humans really at all,

00:06:21.581 --> 00:06:24.116 line:0%
so seeing that bird
being comfortable around us,

00:06:24.116 --> 00:06:27.352 line:0%
and one of the scarlet macaws
was really responding to music

00:06:27.352 --> 00:06:30.122 line:0%
and dancing, it was
a really special moment.

00:06:30.122 --> 00:06:31.490 line:0%
EMILY: What are you doing?

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:32.725 line:0%
Oh!

00:06:32.725 --> 00:06:34.260 line:0%
Oh, my.

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:36.395 line:0%
You're cute.

00:06:36.395 --> 00:06:37.697 line:0%
You guys like Adele.

00:06:37.697 --> 00:06:40.399 line:0%
I understand. I like Adele, too.

00:06:40.399 --> 00:06:42.034 line:0%
First glance at
all of these birds,

00:06:42.034 --> 00:06:43.902 line:0%
they don't look too terrible,

00:06:43.902 --> 00:06:47.639 line:0%
but it's hard to tell without
doing a proper vet exam.

00:06:47.639 --> 00:06:49.075 line:0%
Hi.

00:06:49.075 --> 00:06:51.810 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Macaws, like many
 other exotic animals,</i>

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:54.513 line:0%
<i> mask their illness
 as a way to survive.</i>

00:06:54.513 --> 00:06:57.183 line:0%
<i> Any bird showing outward signs
 of weakness</i>

00:06:57.183 --> 00:06:58.717 line:0%
<i> is likely very sick.</i>

00:06:58.717 --> 00:07:02.421 line:0%
TERRY: You can see his wing
does not extend on this side

00:07:02.421 --> 00:07:04.757 line:0%
like it does on the other side.

00:07:04.757 --> 00:07:06.926 line:0%
WOUTER: Once we get to the zoo,
we'll get an x-ray of that.

00:07:06.926 --> 00:07:08.294 line:0%
It's probably an old break.

00:07:08.294 --> 00:07:09.795 line:0%
JOHN: Old break, yeah,
that's what we thought.

00:07:09.795 --> 00:07:11.430 line:0%
HEATHER: This guy's beak
almost looks like

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:13.532 line:0%
his nare was ripped down.

00:07:13.532 --> 00:07:15.768 line:0%
And it doesn't really
look like a prolapse,

00:07:15.768 --> 00:07:18.437 line:0%
because his cloaca is closed up,
um, like it should be,

00:07:18.437 --> 00:07:19.972 line:0%
but it's bulging behind it,

00:07:19.972 --> 00:07:22.174 line:0%
so it could be lots of
scar tissue that built up.

00:07:22.174 --> 00:07:23.408 line:0%
TERRY: And I suspect maybe

00:07:23.408 --> 00:07:25.878 line:0%
it was from an egg binding
or something like that,

00:07:25.878 --> 00:07:27.679 line:0%
the vet said it's very rare.

00:07:27.679 --> 00:07:28.814 line:0%
WOUTER: So he'd be
one of the ones

00:07:28.814 --> 00:07:30.649 line:0%
I would keep an eye on
during transport,

00:07:30.649 --> 00:07:31.984 line:0%
just because he's breathing
a little heavier,

00:07:31.984 --> 00:07:33.285 line:0%
he's got that squinty eye.

00:07:33.285 --> 00:07:35.287 line:0%
Look at the other one,
he's got his eye wide open,

00:07:35.287 --> 00:07:36.922 line:0%
ready to come over and say hi.

00:07:36.922 --> 00:07:38.958 line:0%
EMILY: We keep an eye on you.
WOUTER: Yeah.

00:07:41.059 --> 00:07:42.861 line:0%
EMILY: We don't really know
if they're sick

00:07:42.861 --> 00:07:44.363 line:0%
or their health conditions

00:07:44.363 --> 00:07:47.266 line:0%
until we get a full checkup
by our vet staff.

00:07:47.266 --> 00:07:49.502 line:0%
Right now, we need to treat
each of these birds

00:07:49.502 --> 00:07:51.270 line:0%
as if their life is on the line.

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:52.871 line:0%
They could have
major health issues,

00:07:52.871 --> 00:07:54.874 line:0%
and they could be
in big trouble.

00:07:58.911 --> 00:08:00.379 line:0%
PRIYA: Easy, my love.
WHITNEY: Ready?

00:08:00.379 --> 00:08:02.681 line:0%
PRIYA: Yeah, I got the front.
SUZI: I got the end.

00:08:02.681 --> 00:08:03.548 line:0%
SUZI: Are we out?

00:08:03.548 --> 00:08:05.551 line:0%
WHITNEY: Lower.
PRIYA: He's out.

00:08:07.453 --> 00:08:09.855 line:0%
PRIYA: So we've just arrived
at Ohio State University,

00:08:09.855 --> 00:08:12.090 line:0%
and we're about to do
Emmett's surgery

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:15.127 line:0%
for his joint replacement.

00:08:15.127 --> 00:08:17.163 line:0%
SUZI: It's alright, buddy.

00:08:17.163 --> 00:08:18.531 line:0%
You're alright.

00:08:18.531 --> 00:08:21.099 line:0%
PRIYA: We're just getting ready
to induce his anesthesia,

00:08:21.099 --> 00:08:22.702 line:0%
so we'll go ahead
and inject him,

00:08:22.702 --> 00:08:25.204 line:0%
get him down, stabilize him.

00:08:25.204 --> 00:08:26.939 line:0%
SUZI: You tell me
when you're ready.

00:08:26.939 --> 00:08:29.442 line:0%
PRIYA: Um, we are ready.

00:08:29.442 --> 00:08:31.176 line:0%
SUZI: Hey, buddy.

00:08:31.176 --> 00:08:33.846 line:0%
PRIYA: Emmett has a condition
in which his cartilage

00:08:33.846 --> 00:08:36.882 line:0%
did not develop appropriately
in a section of the bone.

00:08:36.882 --> 00:08:39.018 line:0%
So he doesn't have
a normal joint,

00:08:39.018 --> 00:08:40.452 line:0%
which is obviously very damaging

00:08:40.452 --> 00:08:43.689 line:0%
to a bone that's
constantly in motion.

00:08:43.689 --> 00:08:45.825 line:0%
SUZI: You want him out?
PRIYA: Mm-hmm.

00:08:45.825 --> 00:08:46.825 line:0%
SUZI: You want to come out here?

00:08:46.825 --> 00:08:48.494 line:0%
He's a shooter, remember.

00:08:48.494 --> 00:08:49.794 line:0%
PRIYA: Yeah. Oh, I know.

00:08:49.794 --> 00:08:51.797 line:0%
SUZI: Hey, buddy. Hey.

00:08:51.797 --> 00:08:53.432 line:0%
I don't want to be
right in front of him.

00:08:53.432 --> 00:08:54.700 line:0%
PRIYA: Yeah, no,
don't be in front of him.

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:58.571 line:0%
We are aiming to try and
regenerate that area of the bone

00:08:58.571 --> 00:09:01.106 line:0%
to try and fill it,
cover it in cartilage

00:09:01.106 --> 00:09:03.442 line:0%
and hope that he develops
a normal joint.

00:09:03.442 --> 00:09:05.177 line:0%
SUZI: Okay. Yep.
PRIYA: Ready?

00:09:05.177 --> 00:09:07.580 line:0%
SUZI: Emmett. Yep.
We got it. We got it.

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:08.914 line:0%
PRIYA: Easy, love. Easy, love.
Easy, love.

00:09:08.914 --> 00:09:11.484 line:0%
SUZI: It's okay. It's okay.

00:09:11.484 --> 00:09:13.219 line:0%
I am very nervous.

00:09:13.219 --> 00:09:16.689 line:0%
You know, it's like watching
your child in pain.

00:09:16.689 --> 00:09:18.491 line:0%
It's very nerve-racking for me.

00:09:18.491 --> 00:09:20.026 line:0%
SUZI: You're alright. Good boy.

00:09:20.026 --> 00:09:21.394 line:0%
BRANNON: Here, bub.
You want to come lay?

00:09:21.394 --> 00:09:23.129 line:0%
SUZI: Good boy.

00:09:26.832 --> 00:09:28.100 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Emmett's
 shoulder surgery</i>

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:30.569 line:0%
<i> is common in
 domestic dogs and cats,</i>

00:09:30.569 --> 00:09:32.037 line:0%
<i> but this is the first time</i>

00:09:32.037 --> 00:09:34.507 line:0%
<i> a cheetah is undergoing
 this procedure.</i>

00:09:34.507 --> 00:09:36.075 line:0%
PRIYA: There's a lot
riding on today.

00:09:36.075 --> 00:09:37.476 line:0%
It's never been done before,

00:09:37.476 --> 00:09:40.178 line:0%
so there's an element
of unknown.

00:09:40.178 --> 00:09:42.181 line:0%
And cheetahs are
incredibly unique

00:09:42.181 --> 00:09:44.050 line:0%
with their anatomy
and physiology

00:09:44.050 --> 00:09:46.185 line:0%
and immunology is
so, so different.

00:09:46.185 --> 00:09:47.720 line:0%
We can't predict
what's going to happen,

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:50.055 line:0%
but we really want it to work.

00:09:50.055 --> 00:09:51.057 line:0%
JONATHAN: Yeah, no,
that will work.

00:09:51.057 --> 00:09:52.525 line:0%
PRIYA: Okay.

00:09:52.525 --> 00:09:53.425 line:0%
JONATHAN: I just don't want to
put him in a stress position

00:09:53.425 --> 00:09:55.127 line:0%
for his shoulders.

00:09:55.127 --> 00:09:57.196 line:0%
SUZI: We have fortunately one
of the best orthopedic surgeons

00:09:57.196 --> 00:09:58.497 line:0%
in the world.

00:09:58.497 --> 00:10:01.801 line:0%
Dr. Jonathan Dyce, he's
a miracle man, in my opinion.

00:10:01.801 --> 00:10:06.505 line:0%
If Jonathan takes on the case,
he has confidence he can fix it.

00:10:12.178 --> 00:10:13.745 line:0%
JONATHAN: Okay, are we good?
Looks like it.

00:10:13.745 --> 00:10:16.081 line:0%
ELIZABETH: Yeah. Looks like it.
JONATHAN: Good.

00:10:16.081 --> 00:10:19.084 line:0%
PRIYA: My concern is
that I'm not entirely sure

00:10:19.084 --> 00:10:23.155 line:0%
we know exactly what's going on
until we're in there.

00:10:23.155 --> 00:10:24.623 line:0%
JONATHAN: I'm going
to make an incision,

00:10:24.623 --> 00:10:27.927 line:0%
I need you to try and help come
through the fascia there.

00:10:30.095 --> 00:10:31.997 line:0%
PRIYA: If this procedure
isn't successful,

00:10:31.997 --> 00:10:33.799 line:0%
he may not have
to have it amputated,

00:10:33.799 --> 00:10:36.268 line:0%
that would be worst, worst,
worst-case scenario.

00:10:36.268 --> 00:10:38.170 line:0%
But it certainly may
become a joint

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:39.905 line:0%
that may end up getting
a little bit fused,

00:10:39.905 --> 00:10:43.275 line:0%
and he may lose some function
in that limb.

00:10:43.275 --> 00:10:46.511 line:0%
I think we're all a little,
a little anxious about it.

00:10:46.511 --> 00:10:48.481 line:0%
(sighs)

00:10:49.815 --> 00:10:51.283 line:0%
SUZI: Waiting.

00:10:59.825 --> 00:11:01.927 line:0%
JONATHAN: You got to hold
that for me.

00:11:03.696 --> 00:11:06.198 line:0%
PRIYA: Cheetah Emmett right now
is in the operating room,

00:11:06.198 --> 00:11:07.633 line:0%
and having a look at that joint

00:11:07.633 --> 00:11:09.902 line:0%
and getting ready
to start fixing it.

00:11:09.902 --> 00:11:11.503 line:0%
Obviously we're all
rooting for him,

00:11:11.503 --> 00:11:14.406 line:0%
he's an animal that has
a special place in our heart

00:11:14.406 --> 00:11:15.908 line:0%
from rescuing him as a cub

00:11:15.908 --> 00:11:17.876 line:0%
and treating him
for all of his problems

00:11:17.876 --> 00:11:19.144 line:0%
when he was a little baby.

00:11:19.144 --> 00:11:23.516 line:0%
So, fingers crossed,
we really want this to go well.

00:11:23.516 --> 00:11:25.284 line:0%
JONATHAN: Hey, Priya,
this is interesting.

00:11:25.284 --> 00:11:28.086 line:0%
If you want to lean in here.

00:11:28.086 --> 00:11:29.154 line:0%
Fantastic.

00:11:29.154 --> 00:11:31.057 line:0%
What I was expecting
to find in here

00:11:31.057 --> 00:11:33.659 line:0%
was that all of this tissue
would be loose and fragmented,

00:11:33.659 --> 00:11:34.793 line:0%
and what he's actually done

00:11:34.793 --> 00:11:37.896 line:0%
is a very good job
of spontaneous repair.

00:11:37.896 --> 00:11:40.832 line:0%
All of the cartilage
is also quite solid.

00:11:40.832 --> 00:11:44.069 line:0%
Now this is actually remodeled
very well, spontaneously.

00:11:44.069 --> 00:11:46.005 line:0%
PRIYA: Oh, good. Wonderful.

00:11:46.005 --> 00:11:47.072 line:0%
Oh, Emmett.

00:11:47.072 --> 00:11:48.440 line:0%
It was absolutely amazing,

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:50.876 line:0%
there was no way we could tell
without going in,

00:11:50.876 --> 00:11:53.546 line:0%
but he's healing himself.

00:11:55.280 --> 00:11:56.615 line:0%
Cheetahs being cheetahs,

00:11:56.615 --> 00:11:58.951 line:0%
always quirks and always
a little bit weird.

00:11:58.951 --> 00:12:01.520 line:0%
He actually started to heal
and self-resolve himself,

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:03.522 line:0%
which has never been seen before

00:12:03.522 --> 00:12:08.160 line:0%
and would not be expected
in a dog or a cat.

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:10.395 line:0%
JONATHAN: The news is good.

00:12:10.395 --> 00:12:12.497 line:0%
He's actually healing
remarkably well.

00:12:12.497 --> 00:12:15.033 line:0%
SUZI: Really?

00:12:15.033 --> 00:12:18.103 line:0%
I'm so happy!

00:12:18.103 --> 00:12:19.772 line:0%
PRIYA: It took
everyone by surprise

00:12:19.772 --> 00:12:21.606 line:0%
that we didn't have to go
to major surgery

00:12:21.606 --> 00:12:23.008 line:0%
to fix this problem.

00:12:23.008 --> 00:12:25.444 line:0%
But Emmett's really not
out of the woods yet.

00:12:25.444 --> 00:12:27.847 line:0%
We really haven't seen this
kind of regrowth before,

00:12:27.847 --> 00:12:29.748 line:0%
so we don't know what to expect.

00:12:29.748 --> 00:12:33.452 line:0%
The next steps for Emmett
involve getting him some R&amp;R,

00:12:33.452 --> 00:12:35.788 line:0%
slowly reintroducing him
to exercise,

00:12:35.788 --> 00:12:36.988 line:0%
but the real test is going to be

00:12:36.988 --> 00:12:39.024 line:0%
if we can get Emmett
up to running speed

00:12:39.024 --> 00:12:43.195 line:0%
and out into the yard and doing
what our normal cheetahs do.

00:12:43.195 --> 00:12:44.863 line:0%
SUZI: Can you come here, buddy?

00:12:44.863 --> 00:12:47.032 line:0%
Can you just lay down
for a little bit?

00:12:47.032 --> 00:12:50.168 line:0%
Hey. Hey.

00:12:50.168 --> 00:12:51.703 line:0%
(purring)

00:12:51.703 --> 00:12:54.506 line:0%
I know, you're a good boy.

00:12:54.506 --> 00:12:56.942 line:0%
You're a good boy.

00:12:56.942 --> 00:12:59.511 line:0%
(kiss) I know.

00:12:59.511 --> 00:13:02.747 line:0%
Want to lay down? Come on.
Let's lay down.

00:13:02.747 --> 00:13:04.616 line:0%
Just because he has
a clean bill of health,

00:13:04.616 --> 00:13:06.685 line:0%
there aren't 100% guarantees.

00:13:06.685 --> 00:13:08.721 line:0%
So, everything's pointing
to it will be good,

00:13:08.721 --> 00:13:10.622 line:0%
but we're just going to
have to wait and see.

00:13:10.622 --> 00:13:12.658 line:0%
(dog barking)

00:13:12.658 --> 00:13:15.260 line:0%
He loves laps.

00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:16.261 line:0%
He's my lap cheetah.

00:13:16.261 --> 00:13:17.696 line:0%
PRIYA: You big baby.

00:13:17.696 --> 00:13:20.099 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Emmett will recover
 and regain strength</i>

00:13:20.099 --> 00:13:24.102 line:0%
<i> before attempting to run
 once again at full speed.</i>

00:13:24.102 --> 00:13:26.305 line:0%
SUZI: You did very good.

00:13:26.305 --> 00:13:28.006 line:0%
Yes, you did.

00:13:28.006 --> 00:13:30.876 line:0%
You're my boy.

00:13:30.876 --> 00:13:33.078 line:0%
You're my baby boy.

00:13:33.078 --> 00:13:34.613 line:0%
(purring)

00:13:34.613 --> 00:13:41.219 line:0%
(music)

00:13:41.219 --> 00:13:43.655 line:0%
DEVON: There she goes!

00:13:43.655 --> 00:13:44.857 line:0%
You guys, show them
how it's done!

00:13:44.857 --> 00:13:45.924 line:0%
ELIZABETH: There!

00:13:45.924 --> 00:13:47.726 line:0%
What is mom doing?

00:13:47.726 --> 00:13:49.294 line:0%
DEVON: What do you think?

00:13:49.294 --> 00:13:52.397 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: It's sink or swim
 for twin baby polar bear cubs,</i>

00:13:52.397 --> 00:13:54.900 line:0%
<i> testing the water
 for the first time.</i>

00:13:54.900 --> 00:13:58.871 line:0%
CARRIE: Today we are working on
giving our cubs pool time,

00:13:58.871 --> 00:14:00.839 line:0%
swimming lessons;
they're still learning.

00:14:00.839 --> 00:14:03.742 line:0%
We have been so invested
in Nuniq and Neva

00:14:03.742 --> 00:14:05.176 line:0%
since the moment they were born.

00:14:05.176 --> 00:14:07.178 line:0%
We're so excited
to see them grow,

00:14:07.178 --> 00:14:10.115 line:0%
but also to see their mom,
Aurora, take care of them.

00:14:10.115 --> 00:14:12.651 line:0%
ASHLEY: They are both so weirded
out by her being in the pool.

00:14:12.651 --> 00:14:14.052 line:0%
DEVON: Yeah.
ASHLEY: Like they're hiding.

00:14:14.052 --> 00:14:15.520 line:0%
DEVON: I know.

00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:17.389 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Neva and Nuniq are
 getting swimming lessons</i>

00:14:17.389 --> 00:14:19.624 line:0%
<i> from mama polar bear Aurora.</i>

00:14:19.624 --> 00:14:21.793 line:0%
<i> Once they prove their
 swimming skills here,</i>

00:14:21.793 --> 00:14:25.664 line:0%
<i> they'll take on the deep end
 inside the polar bear habitat.</i>

00:14:27.967 --> 00:14:29.167 line:0%
DEVON: They are getting
more comfortable now.

00:14:29.167 --> 00:14:30.635 line:0%
ASHLEY: Yeah.

00:14:30.635 --> 00:14:32.805 line:0%
DEVON: They're like, alright,
mom's out of the pool.

00:14:32.805 --> 00:14:35.140 line:0%
CARRIE: I just want to see them
go like 'rawr' and jump on it.

00:14:35.140 --> 00:14:36.675 line:0%
ASHLEY: Come on. You can do it!

00:14:36.675 --> 00:14:37.976 line:0%
DEVON: Oh, there we go!

00:14:37.976 --> 00:14:39.044 line:0%
Good job.

00:14:39.044 --> 00:14:40.379 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR:
 Once in the deep pool,</i>

00:14:40.379 --> 00:14:42.548 line:0%
<i> if the bear cubs
 get in over their heads</i>

00:14:42.548 --> 00:14:44.116 line:0%
<i> with mom by their side,</i>

00:14:44.116 --> 00:14:47.285 line:0%
<i> it will be impossible for
 the keepers to intervene.</i>

00:14:47.285 --> 00:14:49.287 line:0%
ASHLEY: They're very cautious
with the water

00:14:49.287 --> 00:14:51.156 line:0%
since they aren't real
comfortable swimmers,

00:14:51.156 --> 00:14:53.058 line:0%
so they're still testing it out.

00:14:53.058 --> 00:14:55.627 line:0%
CARRIE: These baby steps are
so vitally important to the cubs

00:14:55.627 --> 00:14:57.696 line:0%
for their safety,
for the mom's safety.

00:14:57.696 --> 00:14:59.431 line:0%
We just don't want to put
anyone in a situation

00:14:59.431 --> 00:15:00.565 line:0%
where they might get hurt.

00:15:00.565 --> 00:15:02.934 line:0%
We're not in what we call
free contact;

00:15:02.934 --> 00:15:04.803 line:0%
there's always a barrier
between us and the bears.

00:15:04.803 --> 00:15:06.905 line:0%
And even with the cubs,
that's the same.

00:15:06.905 --> 00:15:10.542 line:0%
We have three polar bear cubs
currently, two different moms.

00:15:10.542 --> 00:15:12.277 line:0%
One has twins,
one has a single cub.

00:15:12.277 --> 00:15:14.512 line:0%
And these are actually
the only three polar bear cubs

00:15:14.512 --> 00:15:18.250 line:0%
born this year in zoos
in North America.

00:15:22.287 --> 00:15:23.689 line:0%
(laughs)

00:15:23.689 --> 00:15:24.623 line:0%
DEVON: She's like, wait,
that was kind of fun,

00:15:24.623 --> 00:15:27.826 line:0%
I might do it again.

00:15:27.826 --> 00:15:29.528 line:0%
CARRIE: It's okay.

00:15:32.464 --> 00:15:35.700 line:0%
You're a dirty, dirty bear.

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:37.869 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Finally,
 the twin polar bear cubs</i>

00:15:37.869 --> 00:15:40.506 line:0%
<i> inch their way
 up to the water's edge.</i>

00:15:40.506 --> 00:15:41.706 line:0%
CARRIE: This one's
gonna swim first.

00:15:41.706 --> 00:15:45.177 line:0%
ASHLEY: Yeah, swim out there
and get it.

00:15:45.177 --> 00:15:49.013 line:0%
Good job! Yay!

00:15:49.013 --> 00:15:50.482 line:0%
DEVON: Awesome.
CARRIE: Good job!

00:15:50.482 --> 00:15:51.683 line:0%
ASHLEY: Yay!

00:15:51.683 --> 00:15:54.252 line:0%
CARRIE: Good job! You did it!

00:15:54.252 --> 00:15:55.854 line:0%
Did you just see that,
that she...

00:15:55.854 --> 00:15:58.557 line:0%
She just did that, right?

00:15:58.557 --> 00:16:01.359 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Neva and Nuniq will
 continue swimming lessons</i>

00:16:01.359 --> 00:16:05.330 line:0%
<i> before facing the deep pool
 in the polar bear habitat.</i>

00:16:05.330 --> 00:16:07.132 line:0%
CARRIE: Today's a good day.

00:16:09.501 --> 00:16:11.202 line:0%
JOY: Who's going swimming today?

00:16:11.202 --> 00:16:13.471 line:0%
Is Miss Haleakala
going swimming?

00:16:13.471 --> 00:16:16.674 line:0%
Doo! Hi!

00:16:16.674 --> 00:16:19.111 line:0%
(squawking)
Someone's happy. Are you happy?

00:16:19.111 --> 00:16:22.480 line:0%
Haleakala and Martini,
who has some arthritis,

00:16:22.480 --> 00:16:24.550 line:0%
he had surgery
when he was younger,

00:16:24.550 --> 00:16:25.450 line:0%
we're going to do

00:16:25.450 --> 00:16:26.251 line:0%
some swimming with them

00:16:26.251 --> 00:16:27.485 line:0%
to allow them to do some

00:16:27.485 --> 00:16:29.221 line:0%
nice physical therapy.

00:16:29.221 --> 00:16:31.156 line:0%
And it helps out their joints.

00:16:31.156 --> 00:16:32.491 line:0%
We're going to head back

00:16:32.491 --> 00:16:34.259 line:0%
and lead them over
to the manatee building.

00:16:34.259 --> 00:16:36.795 line:0%
Come on, buddy boy!

00:16:36.795 --> 00:16:38.796 line:0%
Pink dinosaurs crossing through.

00:16:38.796 --> 00:16:40.865 line:0%
Just hold on a moment.

00:16:40.865 --> 00:16:42.334 line:0%
Come on.

00:16:42.334 --> 00:16:46.105 line:0%
We kind of like to joke that
they're pink Velociraptors.

00:16:46.105 --> 00:16:48.640 line:0%
But birds are living dinosaurs.

00:16:48.640 --> 00:16:51.009 line:0%
They have hydrotherapy buddies,

00:16:51.009 --> 00:16:54.079 line:0%
so Martini or one
of the other adult birds

00:16:54.079 --> 00:16:56.648 line:0%
might be a little bit
more at ease

00:16:56.648 --> 00:16:59.718 line:0%
if they have a buddy
to go with them.

00:16:59.718 --> 00:17:00.786 line:0%
Yeah. (squawking)

00:17:00.786 --> 00:17:01.853 line:0%
He never shuts up.

00:17:01.853 --> 00:17:04.523 line:0%
MALLORY: I know,
Tini hasn't made a peep!

00:17:04.523 --> 00:17:06.791 line:0%
JOY: Let's go, buddy,
here we go. Inside.

00:17:06.791 --> 00:17:08.727 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Today's session
 includes a new element</i>

00:17:08.727 --> 00:17:11.496 line:0%
<i> guaranteed to get
 the flamingos moving--</i>

00:17:11.496 --> 00:17:13.532 line:0%
<i> stingrays!</i>

00:17:13.532 --> 00:17:15.066 line:0%
MALLORY: We ready
to go to the pool?

00:17:15.066 --> 00:17:17.903 line:0%
JOY: Ready? Are you ready?
Are you ready? Are you ready?

00:17:17.903 --> 00:17:20.105 line:0%
The large amount of stingrays
in this pool

00:17:20.105 --> 00:17:22.640 line:0%
are up here on vacation.

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:24.876 line:0%
Their habitat is getting
renovated currently.

00:17:24.876 --> 00:17:27.045 line:0%
And so that's just kind of
excellent enrichment

00:17:27.045 --> 00:17:28.313 line:0%
for the flamingos.

00:17:28.313 --> 00:17:29.982 line:0%
They're going to be extra aware
of their environment

00:17:29.982 --> 00:17:32.150 line:0%
and the new things that are
moving underneath their feet.

00:17:32.150 --> 00:17:33.886 line:0%
Hopefully it will get them
moving a little bit more,

00:17:33.886 --> 00:17:34.953 line:0%
which is great.

00:17:34.953 --> 00:17:37.523 line:0%
Are you ready to meet stingrays?

00:17:37.523 --> 00:17:38.823 line:0%
(squawking)

00:17:38.823 --> 00:17:40.491 line:0%
And then also the stingrays
are getting enriched,

00:17:40.491 --> 00:17:41.760 line:0%
because I don't think

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:44.329 line:0%
they've ever seen
a flamingo before either.

00:17:44.329 --> 00:17:47.332 line:0%
You're gonna swim
with the stingrays!

00:17:47.332 --> 00:17:48.834 line:0%
I think she sees them,
she sees them.

00:17:48.834 --> 00:17:50.269 line:0%
(squawking)

00:17:50.269 --> 00:17:53.839 line:0%
I think the stingrays are going
to probably be circling around

00:17:53.839 --> 00:17:55.574 line:0%
just kind of investigating.

00:17:55.574 --> 00:17:59.010 line:0%
But I don't think that they're
going to be getting too brave

00:17:59.010 --> 00:18:00.312 line:0%
with the flamingos.

00:18:00.312 --> 00:18:03.448 line:0%
We trim their barbs
so they don't have a response

00:18:03.448 --> 00:18:04.817 line:0%
that would trigger anything,

00:18:04.817 --> 00:18:06.384 line:0%
and we keep an eye on everybody

00:18:06.384 --> 00:18:08.352 line:0%
to make sure that
they're all comfortable.

00:18:08.352 --> 00:18:10.656 line:0%
Let me grab Haleakala.

00:18:13.225 --> 00:18:15.427 line:0%
Oh! There we are!

00:18:15.427 --> 00:18:16.528 line:0%
(squawking)

00:18:16.528 --> 00:18:19.364 line:0%
What you think? What you think?

00:18:19.364 --> 00:18:24.569 line:0%
I think she is back happy
in the pool again.

00:18:24.569 --> 00:18:26.404 line:0%
Once we get the flamingos
in the water,

00:18:26.404 --> 00:18:29.340 line:0%
they immediately get
their feet moving.

00:18:29.340 --> 00:18:30.842 line:0%
Stingray!

00:18:30.842 --> 00:18:32.143 line:0%
Did you see the stingrays?

00:18:32.143 --> 00:18:34.045 line:0%
Look, they're
underneath you now.

00:18:34.045 --> 00:18:35.413 line:0%
They get a little bit
more alert,

00:18:35.413 --> 00:18:36.849 line:0%
which is actually pretty good,

00:18:36.849 --> 00:18:39.484 line:0%
because then it will encourage
them to swim a little bit more.

00:18:39.484 --> 00:18:41.286 line:0%
Do you see stingrays?

00:18:41.286 --> 00:18:42.287 line:0%
They're underneath!

00:18:42.287 --> 00:18:43.488 line:0%
Look underneath.

00:18:43.488 --> 00:18:48.894 line:0%
(music)

00:18:48.894 --> 00:18:52.897 line:0%
So she's just learned that she
can stick her head underwater

00:18:52.897 --> 00:18:54.900 line:0%
and check out underneath.

00:18:57.735 --> 00:19:00.572 line:0%
When you see flamingos, they're
iconic for standing on one leg,

00:19:00.572 --> 00:19:01.907 line:0%
which is actually

00:19:01.907 --> 00:19:04.142 line:0%
an energy-saving mechanism
for them,

00:19:04.142 --> 00:19:06.277 line:0%
so hydrotherapy is
very important,

00:19:06.277 --> 00:19:08.980 line:0%
because to actually get them
to do exercise,

00:19:08.980 --> 00:19:10.949 line:0%
they don't normally
do that very often.

00:19:10.949 --> 00:19:12.651 line:0%
Encouraging them
to move around a lot

00:19:12.651 --> 00:19:14.653 line:0%
is basically
therapeutic for them

00:19:14.653 --> 00:19:17.923 line:0%
and getting them to use their
muscles in an encouraging way.

00:19:17.923 --> 00:19:20.525 line:0%
MALLORY: Hydrotherapy! Woo!

00:19:20.525 --> 00:19:22.227 line:0%
JOY: Woo-hoo!

00:19:22.227 --> 00:19:24.629 line:0%
MALLORY: I think that was a very
successful hydrotherapy session.

00:19:24.629 --> 00:19:26.331 line:0%
JOY: It's another
'I love my job' day.

00:19:26.331 --> 00:19:30.736 line:0%
We have a lot of those.
Don't we? Don't we?

00:19:30.736 --> 00:19:33.472 line:0%
Yes, we do. Boop!

00:19:35.373 --> 00:19:38.943 line:0%
ZACK: Morning, guys.

00:19:38.943 --> 00:19:40.279 line:0%
Morning, Goby.

00:19:40.279 --> 00:19:41.813 line:0%
Feeling alright today, big man?

00:19:41.813 --> 00:19:44.883 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: 90 miles away
 at the zoo's conservation park,</i>

00:19:44.883 --> 00:19:48.152 line:0%
<i> the animals are about to get
 their daily check-in from Zack.</i>

00:19:48.152 --> 00:19:49.988 line:0%
ZACK: Hey, Tuffy.
Hey, over here.

00:19:49.988 --> 00:19:52.157 line:0%
My main responsibilities
here at The Wilds

00:19:52.157 --> 00:19:53.625 line:0%
is to take care
of our pasture area.

00:19:53.625 --> 00:19:54.859 line:0%
We just try to make sure

00:19:54.859 --> 00:19:56.060 line:0%
everybody's where
they're supposed to be.

00:19:56.060 --> 00:19:58.063 line:0%
We'll check any
of our medical cases.

00:19:58.063 --> 00:20:00.598 line:0%
Also, we have a couple animals
that could be expecting births,

00:20:00.598 --> 00:20:02.100 line:0%
and we'll try to
check on those animals

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:05.170 line:0%
and make sure we don't have
any surprises in the morning.

00:20:06.804 --> 00:20:09.107 line:0%
Good morning.
How's it going, Flicka?

00:20:09.107 --> 00:20:11.876 line:0%
What are you doing
out here by yourself?

00:20:11.876 --> 00:20:13.478 line:0%
You feeling alright, girl?

00:20:13.478 --> 00:20:16.147 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Flicka, a female
 Przewalski horse,</i>

00:20:16.147 --> 00:20:18.883 line:0%
<i> is all alone,
 separated from the herd,</i>

00:20:18.883 --> 00:20:20.619 line:0%
<i> which is cause for concern.</i>

00:20:20.619 --> 00:20:23.087 line:0%
ZACK: Anytime for me, I see
this, this is a key indicator.

00:20:23.087 --> 00:20:25.256 line:0%
I need to make sure
this animal is okay.

00:20:25.256 --> 00:20:26.724 line:0%
Check for lameness,

00:20:26.724 --> 00:20:31.863 line:0%
check for any wounds on her,
demeanor changes.

00:20:31.863 --> 00:20:33.798 line:0%
Seeing her away
from the group like that

00:20:33.798 --> 00:20:35.199 line:0%
is definitely a red flag.

00:20:35.199 --> 00:20:38.403 line:0%
These are those small cues that
if you can nip it in the bud

00:20:38.403 --> 00:20:41.973 line:0%
really quick, you can really
save a really bad situation.

00:20:41.973 --> 00:20:45.076 line:0%
These guys are
extremely rare animals.

00:20:45.076 --> 00:20:47.512 line:0%
These horses are one of the most
primitive horse species

00:20:47.512 --> 00:20:48.713 line:0%
you can find.

00:20:48.713 --> 00:20:50.916 line:0%
This animal almost
went extinct in the wild,

00:20:50.916 --> 00:20:52.550 line:0%
and so just the loss of one

00:20:52.550 --> 00:20:55.387 line:0%
would be a horrible thing
for us.

00:20:59.657 --> 00:21:01.292 line:0%
ZACK: Good morning.
How's it going, Flicka?

00:21:01.292 --> 00:21:04.329 line:0%
What are you doing
out here by yourself?

00:21:04.329 --> 00:21:06.030 line:0%
You feeling alright, girl?

00:21:06.030 --> 00:21:08.499 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Flicka,
 a female Przewalski horse</i>

00:21:08.499 --> 00:21:10.102 line:0%
<i> is all by herself.</i>

00:21:10.102 --> 00:21:12.103 line:0%
<i> The herd is nowhere in sight.</i>

00:21:12.103 --> 00:21:13.538 line:0%
(neigh)

00:21:13.538 --> 00:21:15.607 line:0%
ZACK: You can see she's neighing
for the rest of the group.

00:21:15.607 --> 00:21:17.976 line:0%
Horses, come on!

00:21:17.976 --> 00:21:19.745 line:0%
Here they come.

00:21:21.379 --> 00:21:23.282 line:0%
Come on, girls.

00:21:25.049 --> 00:21:26.485 line:0%
(whinny)

00:21:30.121 --> 00:21:31.189 line:0%
(whinny)

00:21:31.189 --> 00:21:32.757 line:0%
Within the herd,
there's a pecking order.

00:21:32.757 --> 00:21:34.493 line:0%
They usually have
a couple animals on top

00:21:34.493 --> 00:21:37.095 line:0%
that are dominant, and then you
got a couple on the lower end

00:21:37.095 --> 00:21:40.565 line:0%
that are just
kind of subordinates.

00:21:40.565 --> 00:21:42.200 line:0%
Flicka, she's kind of
the outsider.

00:21:42.200 --> 00:21:44.068 line:0%
A lot of times
she's away from the group,

00:21:44.068 --> 00:21:46.170 line:0%
but she was off by herself
a little more than usual

00:21:46.170 --> 00:21:47.939 line:0%
and a little bit
further distances.

00:21:47.939 --> 00:21:49.841 line:0%
So we're going to have the vets
take a quick look at her

00:21:49.841 --> 00:21:52.077 line:0%
and just make sure
everything looks OK.

00:21:52.077 --> 00:21:53.178 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Flicka will come in</i>

00:21:53.178 --> 00:21:56.081 line:0%
<i> for a full examination
 later today.</i>

00:21:58.817 --> 00:22:00.252 line:0%
(birds chirping)

00:22:00.252 --> 00:22:03.689 line:0%
<i> In Alabama, macaws saved
 from a hoarding situation</i>

00:22:03.689 --> 00:22:06.924 line:0%
<i> are about to begin their
 long journey back to Ohio,</i>

00:22:06.924 --> 00:22:09.961 line:0%
<i> where the zoo doctors are
 waiting to examine the birds</i>

00:22:09.961 --> 00:22:12.063 line:0%
<i> and give them a new life.</i>

00:22:12.063 --> 00:22:13.765 line:0%
WOUTER: Emily, if you don't mind
coming in here with me,

00:22:13.765 --> 00:22:15.700 line:0%
just in case
I get feet or a beak.

00:22:15.700 --> 00:22:16.667 line:0%
EMILY: Mm-hmm, yep.

00:22:16.667 --> 00:22:19.137 line:0%
Macaw beaks are
very intimidating.

00:22:19.137 --> 00:22:20.571 line:0%
We're just going to have
to be really careful

00:22:20.571 --> 00:22:22.340 line:0%
when we're maneuvering
them around,

00:22:22.340 --> 00:22:23.641 line:0%
because we could get bit.

00:22:23.641 --> 00:22:24.943 line:0%
It could get hairy.

00:22:24.943 --> 00:22:25.910 line:0%
WOUTER: Hey, bubba.

00:22:25.910 --> 00:22:28.480 line:0%
(squawk)

00:22:28.480 --> 00:22:30.582 line:0%
Come here. (squawk)
Oh, buddy, I know.

00:22:30.582 --> 00:22:31.916 line:0%
EMILY: They don't know that
we're here to help them.

00:22:31.916 --> 00:22:33.184 line:0%
That's the tricky part,

00:22:33.184 --> 00:22:34.552 line:0%
is like you want to be able
to tell them, like,

00:22:34.552 --> 00:22:35.953 line:0%
'Just get in the crate.
It'll be fine.

00:22:35.953 --> 00:22:37.389 line:0%
We're going to take you
to Columbus.'

00:22:37.389 --> 00:22:40.091 line:0%
But, unfortunately, we can't
really communicate with them,

00:22:40.091 --> 00:22:42.027 line:0%
so we're just going to move
as quick as possible.

00:22:42.027 --> 00:22:43.461 line:0%
That's the best thing
we can do for them,

00:22:43.461 --> 00:22:44.829 line:0%
and hopefully build that trust

00:22:44.829 --> 00:22:47.732 line:0%
so they can realize
we're the good guys.

00:22:47.732 --> 00:22:49.768 line:0%
(squawking)

00:22:49.768 --> 00:22:55.073 line:0%
(squawking)

00:22:55.073 --> 00:22:56.341 line:0%
WOUTER: Woo!

00:22:56.341 --> 00:22:58.009 line:0%
He's chunky.

00:22:58.009 --> 00:22:59.710 line:0%
EMILY: You're like a ninja.

00:22:59.710 --> 00:23:01.612 line:0%
HEATHER: That was so nice!

00:23:01.612 --> 00:23:03.348 line:0%
WOUTER: I feel like
a waiter right now.

00:23:03.348 --> 00:23:04.850 line:0%
Um, could I take your order,
please?

00:23:04.850 --> 00:23:06.151 line:0%
EMILY: May I have two hyacinths?

00:23:06.151 --> 00:23:07.419 line:0%
WOUTER: Two hyacinths?
EMILY: Yes, please.

00:23:07.419 --> 00:23:08.786 line:0%
WOUTER: OK. What do you want?
Males or females?

00:23:08.786 --> 00:23:10.788 line:0%
EMILY: I want all of them.

00:23:10.788 --> 00:23:13.691 line:0%
WOUTER: Hi, bubba,
I'm so sorry for all this.

00:23:13.691 --> 00:23:14.825 line:0%
You're really worried
about their beak.

00:23:14.825 --> 00:23:16.728 line:0%
They hold on and
then they grind.

00:23:16.728 --> 00:23:18.530 line:0%
So always get the head first.

00:23:18.530 --> 00:23:19.898 line:0%
As soon as you can get the head,
you've got to be careful,

00:23:19.898 --> 00:23:21.699 line:0%
you don't want the head
to be loose

00:23:21.699 --> 00:23:22.934 line:0%
while the body is
swinging around,

00:23:22.934 --> 00:23:25.370 line:0%
so it's kind of a double grab.

00:23:25.370 --> 00:23:26.370 line:0%
There you go.

00:23:26.370 --> 00:23:27.572 line:0%
Inside.

00:23:27.572 --> 00:23:29.074 line:0%
Nice job.

00:23:29.074 --> 00:23:30.041 line:0%
EMILY: Waah!

00:23:30.041 --> 00:23:31.175 line:0%
(phone ringing)

00:23:31.175 --> 00:23:33.111 line:0%
SUZI: Hello.
WOUTER: Hello, how are you?

00:23:33.111 --> 00:23:33.912 line:0%
SUZI: Good.

00:23:33.912 --> 00:23:35.113 line:0%
WOUTER: We just loaded them up.

00:23:35.113 --> 00:23:38.316 line:0%
One of them has a leg
that is swollen and hot.

00:23:38.316 --> 00:23:40.084 line:0%
And there's one that
just doesn't look like

00:23:40.084 --> 00:23:41.853 line:0%
it's breathing just 100%,

00:23:41.853 --> 00:23:43.488 line:0%
we might want to get
the vets to look at

00:23:43.488 --> 00:23:46.457 line:0%
sooner better than later,
so maybe tomorrow.

00:23:46.457 --> 00:23:48.726 line:0%
SUZI: As soon, you know,
shortly when they get in here

00:23:48.726 --> 00:23:51.263 line:0%
and then they plan on doing
full work-ups on them,

00:23:51.263 --> 00:23:52.830 line:0%
because we don't know anything.

00:23:52.830 --> 00:23:54.399 line:0%
WOUTER: Good.
SUZI: Good job, good job.

00:23:54.399 --> 00:23:58.102 line:0%
WOUTER: Hey, we're not
there yet, halfway there.

00:23:58.102 --> 00:24:00.372 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The macaws are
 on their way to Columbus</i>

00:24:00.372 --> 00:24:04.409 line:0%
<i> where the zoo team anxiously
 awaits their arrival.</i>

00:24:04.409 --> 00:24:05.710 line:0%
EMILY: Bye-bye.
BIRD: Bye-bye.

00:24:05.710 --> 00:24:07.212 line:0%
EMILY: Bye-bye.

00:24:07.212 --> 00:24:10.314 line:0%
WOUTER: We're on our way to
Columbus, get out of our way.

00:24:10.314 --> 00:24:13.285 line:0%
HEATHER: Now just
12 short hours.

00:24:19.758 --> 00:24:20.859 line:0%
ZACK: Today we're going
to bring in

00:24:20.859 --> 00:24:23.928 line:0%
our Przewalski wild horse
female herd.

00:24:23.928 --> 00:24:26.030 line:0%
I noticed that Flicka
was off by herself

00:24:26.030 --> 00:24:27.265 line:0%
a little more than usual.

00:24:27.265 --> 00:24:28.700 line:0%
Maybe something
might be off with her.

00:24:28.700 --> 00:24:30.568 line:0%
So we're gonna have the vets
take a quick look at her

00:24:30.568 --> 00:24:32.403 line:0%
and just make sure
everything looks OK.

00:24:32.403 --> 00:24:34.139 line:0%
Since we're gonna go ahead
and bring her in,

00:24:34.139 --> 00:24:35.273 line:0%
we're gonna bring
the whole herd in.

00:24:35.273 --> 00:24:37.241 line:0%
Anytime you have an animal
within a herd,

00:24:37.241 --> 00:24:38.776 line:0%
you don't want to remove it
from that herd,

00:24:38.776 --> 00:24:40.245 line:0%
it's gonna put things
in disarray,

00:24:40.245 --> 00:24:41.879 line:0%
everybody's going to be
high stress levels

00:24:41.879 --> 00:24:43.114 line:0%
and really worried.

00:24:43.114 --> 00:24:46.350 line:0%
So by keeping the herd together
as we take her in,

00:24:46.350 --> 00:24:47.618 line:0%
is the best thing for it.

00:24:47.618 --> 00:24:49.254 line:0%
CASEY: Here she comes.

00:24:52.757 --> 00:24:54.091 line:0%
ZACK: Who's getting the blood?

00:24:54.091 --> 00:24:55.026 line:0%
KRYSTIN: I'm going to try.

00:24:55.026 --> 00:24:55.927 line:0%
ZACK: Krystin, woo!

00:24:55.927 --> 00:24:56.728 line:0%
PATRICK: Nice, Squeaks.

00:24:56.728 --> 00:24:57.695 line:0%
ZACK: The vampire!

00:24:57.695 --> 00:24:58.530 line:0%
PATRICK: Nice.

00:24:58.530 --> 00:24:59.797 line:0%
ZACK: The vampire!

00:24:59.797 --> 00:25:01.399 line:0%
KRYSTIN: You guys have all
jinxed me now.

00:25:01.399 --> 00:25:02.534 line:0%
(laughs)

00:25:02.534 --> 00:25:03.735 line:0%
ZACK: You've got to work
under pressure.

00:25:03.735 --> 00:25:05.136 line:0%
CURT: We are trying to
get blood off Flicka.

00:25:05.136 --> 00:25:06.771 line:0%
She's been acting just
a little bit different.

00:25:06.771 --> 00:25:09.407 line:0%
Due to her age,
she's born around 2000, 2001.

00:25:09.407 --> 00:25:10.475 line:0%
We just want to get some blood

00:25:10.475 --> 00:25:12.477 line:0%
and see how everything
is going on.

00:25:12.477 --> 00:25:14.445 line:0%
PATRICK: Look at that,
freakin' nailed it.

00:25:14.445 --> 00:25:15.514 line:0%
ELLEN: We'll take a look

00:25:15.514 --> 00:25:18.516 line:0%
and see if we can
see anything more.

00:25:18.516 --> 00:25:19.984 line:0%
Yeah, nothing obvious.

00:25:19.984 --> 00:25:21.719 line:0%
CURT: Check her eyes, too.

00:25:21.719 --> 00:25:23.154 line:0%
ELLEN: Check her eyes?

00:25:23.154 --> 00:25:24.589 line:0%
CURT: Yeah, she kind of had
a hard time finding the holes

00:25:24.589 --> 00:25:26.324 line:0%
back there
that she usually does.

00:25:26.324 --> 00:25:27.392 line:0%
ELLEN: OK, can I get around you?

00:25:27.392 --> 00:25:28.860 line:0%
Is that alright or is that...

00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:30.328 line:0%
No, OK, I'll work from here.

00:25:30.328 --> 00:25:32.430 line:0%
ZACK: Yeah, try, I just don't
want you to get bit.

00:25:33.765 --> 00:25:35.366 line:0%
Argh!

00:25:35.366 --> 00:25:37.635 line:0%
Alright,
alright, alright.

00:25:37.635 --> 00:25:40.271 line:0%
(clattering)

00:25:40.271 --> 00:25:41.373 line:0%
(neighs)

00:25:46.043 --> 00:25:47.945 line:0%
ZACK: We're bringing in Flicka
and get her checked out.

00:25:47.945 --> 00:25:49.780 line:0%
That has become our priority.

00:25:49.780 --> 00:25:52.016 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Flicka is
 a 16-year-old mare</i>

00:25:52.016 --> 00:25:54.352 line:0%
<i> that's not too happy
 about having a checkup.</i>

00:25:54.352 --> 00:25:56.755 line:0%
ZACK: Yeah, try, I just don't
want you to get bit.

00:25:56.755 --> 00:25:58.556 line:0%
Argh!

00:25:58.556 --> 00:25:59.457 line:0%
Alright, alright, alright.

00:25:59.457 --> 00:26:02.060 line:0%
(neighs)

00:26:02.060 --> 00:26:05.196 line:0%
ELLEN: She's not
very relaxed, obviously.

00:26:05.196 --> 00:26:07.065 line:0%
ZACK: Yeah, try, I just don't
want you to get bit.

00:26:07.065 --> 00:26:08.533 line:0%
ELLEN: No, I don't want
to get bit either.

00:26:08.533 --> 00:26:11.736 line:0%
Relax, my friend, relax.

00:26:11.736 --> 00:26:14.705 line:0%
Oh, I think we may have
a cataract forming.

00:26:14.705 --> 00:26:18.376 line:0%
This animal has a cataract
forming in its left eye.

00:26:18.376 --> 00:26:20.678 line:0%
Eye issues are really
painful sometimes,

00:26:20.678 --> 00:26:22.379 line:0%
and when animals
are uncomfortable,

00:26:22.379 --> 00:26:24.815 line:0%
they often will separate
themselves from the herd.

00:26:24.815 --> 00:26:27.585 line:0%
So I think it's definitely
one of the possibilities

00:26:27.585 --> 00:26:30.055 line:0%
as to why she's keeping
herself apart.

00:26:30.055 --> 00:26:31.656 line:0%
That eye looks better
than that eye.

00:26:31.656 --> 00:26:32.557 line:0%
A lot of change, though.

00:26:32.557 --> 00:26:33.358 line:0%
DAN: OK, are we done with her?

00:26:33.358 --> 00:26:35.426 line:0%
ELLEN: We're done.

00:26:35.426 --> 00:26:37.095 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Luckily, Flicka's eye</i>

00:26:37.095 --> 00:26:39.597 line:0%
<i> does not require
 immediate attention.</i>

00:26:39.597 --> 00:26:43.068 line:0%
<i> If it gets worse, the staff
 will bring her in ASAP.</i>

00:26:43.068 --> 00:26:45.436 line:0%
<i> If her blood test
 comes back OK,</i>

00:26:45.436 --> 00:26:49.673 line:0%
<i> herd politics may be
 Flicka's last hurdle.</i>

00:26:49.673 --> 00:26:51.642 line:0%
ZACK: Now that we've brought
the whole herd in,

00:26:51.642 --> 00:26:53.645 line:0%
this may be a chance for things
to mix up a little bit

00:26:53.645 --> 00:26:54.646 line:0%
within the herd,

00:26:54.646 --> 00:26:56.647 line:0%
so Flicka will have
the opportunity

00:26:56.647 --> 00:26:58.049 line:0%
to reestablish herself.

00:26:58.049 --> 00:26:59.217 line:0%
It will take a couple days,

00:26:59.217 --> 00:27:01.419 line:0%
and we'll be able to see
where Flicka ends up,

00:27:01.419 --> 00:27:03.455 line:0%
if she goes back to
the same behavior she's had

00:27:03.455 --> 00:27:05.423 line:0%
for the last couple days or so,

00:27:05.423 --> 00:27:07.926 line:0%
or if she gets back
to normal again.

00:27:11.562 --> 00:27:17.068 line:0%
(music)

00:27:17.068 --> 00:27:18.937 line:0%
SUZI: I'm really excited
for you to see him.

00:27:18.937 --> 00:27:20.271 line:0%
PRIYA: Good!

00:27:20.271 --> 00:27:22.840 line:0%
SUZI: Emmett had a big, gaping
hole in his shoulder,

00:27:22.840 --> 00:27:25.843 line:0%
so we didn't know if he would
ever be able to run.

00:27:25.843 --> 00:27:27.645 line:0%
So Priya's gonna come over
and check him.

00:27:27.645 --> 00:27:29.647 line:0%
And we're really hopeful

00:27:29.647 --> 00:27:32.349 line:0%
that we get to start
running Emmett again.

00:27:32.349 --> 00:27:33.551 line:0%
PRIYA: He looks great.

00:27:33.551 --> 00:27:35.620 line:0%
SUZI: Doesn't he look good?
PRIYA: Yeah!

00:27:35.620 --> 00:27:37.655 line:0%
PRIYA: Will he let me
have a feel and just see?

00:27:37.655 --> 00:27:39.357 line:0%
SUZI: I think he will.
PRIYA: OK.

00:27:39.357 --> 00:27:41.926 line:0%
SUZI: Can Priya check your leg?

00:27:41.926 --> 00:27:43.627 line:0%
Can she check your leg?

00:27:43.627 --> 00:27:44.929 line:0%
Good boy.

00:27:44.929 --> 00:27:46.564 line:0%
PRIYA: Hi, bud. Hi, handsome.

00:27:46.564 --> 00:27:48.032 line:0%
SUZI: What do you think?
PRIYA: Hi, bud.

00:27:48.032 --> 00:27:50.401 line:0%
SUZI: What do you think? Oh.

00:27:50.401 --> 00:27:52.370 line:0%
Well...
(laughs)

00:27:52.370 --> 00:27:54.772 line:0%
PRIYA: It feels great.
It feels very solid.

00:27:54.772 --> 00:27:57.242 line:0%
There's no swelling in there,
there's no fluid in there.

00:27:57.242 --> 00:27:58.643 line:0%
SUZI: Think we can run him?

00:27:58.643 --> 00:28:00.077 line:0%
PRIYA: Yeah?

00:28:00.077 --> 00:28:03.047 line:0%
It gives me anxiety,
but yeah, I think he's ready.

00:28:03.047 --> 00:28:04.849 line:0%
The run will be the moment
of truth, so...

00:28:04.849 --> 00:28:05.683 line:0%
SUZI: Let's do it.

00:28:05.683 --> 00:28:06.885 line:0%
PRIYA: Let's see how he does.

00:28:11.288 --> 00:28:13.491 line:0%
SUZI: Now it's time
for us to see

00:28:13.491 --> 00:28:17.661 line:0%
if Emmett can do
the cheetah run.

00:28:17.661 --> 00:28:20.465 line:0%
Ew, it's awfully wet.

00:28:20.465 --> 00:28:22.066 line:0%
WHITNEY: Yeah, that doesn't
look good.

00:28:22.066 --> 00:28:23.001 line:0%
SUZI: Look at this.

00:28:23.001 --> 00:28:25.736 line:0%
Yeah, I'm not liking what I see.

00:28:25.736 --> 00:28:28.106 line:0%
Two days of rain
doesn't help us.

00:28:28.106 --> 00:28:30.341 line:0%
I don't want to take
any chances with him,

00:28:30.341 --> 00:28:32.343 line:0%
after all he's been through.

00:28:32.343 --> 00:28:34.411 line:0%
Well, let's not do it,

00:28:34.411 --> 00:28:36.447 line:0%
let's see if we can get them
to bring him out.

00:28:36.447 --> 00:28:38.682 line:0%
Let's just let him play.

00:28:38.682 --> 00:28:40.518 line:0%
WHITNEY: Alright, Emily,
we're ready for Emmett,

00:28:40.518 --> 00:28:42.987 line:0%
you can bring him down.

00:28:42.987 --> 00:28:45.056 line:0%
EMILY: Copy, on the way.

00:28:45.056 --> 00:28:47.825 line:0%
SUZI: Here he comes.
Oh! (laughs)

00:28:47.825 --> 00:28:49.460 line:0%
Hey, buddy.

00:28:49.460 --> 00:28:51.128 line:0%
What do you think?

00:28:51.128 --> 00:28:52.830 line:0%
I think he's a little nervous.

00:28:52.830 --> 00:28:55.900 line:0%
I think it might be good
to get Cullen down here.

00:28:55.900 --> 00:28:56.868 line:0%
EMILY: Make him feel
more comfortable?

00:28:56.868 --> 00:28:58.136 line:0%
SUZI: Yeah.

00:28:58.136 --> 00:28:59.704 line:0%
We still noticed Emmett
might be a little skittish,

00:28:59.704 --> 00:29:01.139 line:0%
so we're gonna bring
Cullen down,

00:29:01.139 --> 00:29:04.375 line:0%
that's his dog that
he believes is his brother,

00:29:04.375 --> 00:29:06.210 line:0%
and that's gonna give him
the confidence

00:29:06.210 --> 00:29:08.713 line:0%
he didn't have before
he walked in here.

00:29:08.713 --> 00:29:10.348 line:0%
EMILY: Here he is.
SUZI: Nervous.

00:29:10.348 --> 00:29:14.151 line:0%
EMILY: Come here, Cullen.
Where is he?

00:29:14.151 --> 00:29:17.021 line:0%
SUZI: There they go. (laughs)

00:29:17.021 --> 00:29:18.856 line:0%
There's the confidence.

00:29:18.856 --> 00:29:20.992 line:0%
(laughs)

00:29:20.992 --> 00:29:23.193 line:0%
Look at these two.

00:29:23.193 --> 00:29:24.528 line:0%
He was obviously
more comfortable

00:29:24.528 --> 00:29:25.830 line:0%
when Cullen got down.

00:29:25.830 --> 00:29:28.333 line:0%
Let's go ahead and
send Cash and Coby.

00:29:30.501 --> 00:29:33.204 line:0%
We have two other dogs
that are cheetah dogs.

00:29:33.204 --> 00:29:35.673 line:0%
Emmett got to be friends
with all of them.

00:29:35.673 --> 00:29:38.276 line:0%
Ooh, here he goes.

00:29:38.276 --> 00:29:39.744 line:0%
Look at him jump.

00:29:39.744 --> 00:29:42.946 line:0%
I'm not sure if those three
think they're a cheetah,

00:29:42.946 --> 00:29:46.150 line:0%
or Emmett thinks
he's a yellow Lab.

00:29:46.150 --> 00:29:48.953 line:0%
Look at him. Ooh! Oh!
(laughs)

00:29:48.953 --> 00:29:50.621 line:0%
Don't hurt that shoulder, buddy.

00:29:50.621 --> 00:29:52.257 line:0%
Oh!

00:29:52.257 --> 00:29:53.591 line:0%
EMILY: Well, I think
we made a good choice

00:29:53.591 --> 00:29:55.559 line:0%
in calling
the cheetah run today.

00:29:55.559 --> 00:29:57.395 line:0%
SUZI: Yeah.
Did you see him slide?

00:29:57.395 --> 00:29:58.963 line:0%
Emmett did beautifully today.

00:29:58.963 --> 00:30:01.299 line:0%
He got out here,
he explored the area.

00:30:01.299 --> 00:30:03.400 line:0%
He didn't limp
at the end of the day.

00:30:03.400 --> 00:30:07.806 line:0%
The next step is the run, and I
can't wait to see that guy run.

00:30:10.441 --> 00:30:14.145 line:0%
(music)

00:30:14.145 --> 00:30:15.947 line:0%
CARRIE: Carrie to Ellie.

00:30:15.947 --> 00:30:17.815 line:0%
ELLIE: Go ahead.

00:30:17.815 --> 00:30:19.583 line:0%
CARRIE: We're ready for bears.

00:30:19.583 --> 00:30:20.718 line:0%
ELLIE: I copy.

00:30:20.718 --> 00:30:22.453 line:0%
I'm going to be shifting
Aurora, Nuniq, and Neva

00:30:22.453 --> 00:30:24.889 line:0%
out into the main yard.

00:30:24.889 --> 00:30:27.257 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: It's time for
 Aurora's twin polar bear cubs</i>

00:30:27.257 --> 00:30:30.261 line:0%
<i> to take their first plunge
 into the habitat pool,</i>

00:30:30.261 --> 00:30:33.564 line:0%
<i> while all of
 the zoo-goers watch.</i>

00:30:33.564 --> 00:30:34.799 line:0%
CARRIE: Getting to this point

00:30:34.799 --> 00:30:36.801 line:0%
has been actually
years in the making.

00:30:36.801 --> 00:30:38.936 line:0%
We spent so much time
making sure

00:30:38.936 --> 00:30:40.571 line:0%
that they're strong
enough to swim,

00:30:40.571 --> 00:30:42.173 line:0%
that they wouldn't
have any fear.

00:30:42.173 --> 00:30:44.208 line:0%
When they are out here
standing on that big rock,

00:30:44.208 --> 00:30:46.410 line:0%
I can't help but be a little
nervous, a little anxious,

00:30:46.410 --> 00:30:48.880 line:0%
just hoping that everything
is gonna go OK.

00:30:51.649 --> 00:30:53.684 line:0%
(cheering)

00:30:53.684 --> 00:30:58.890 line:0%
(music)

00:30:58.890 --> 00:31:01.292 line:0%
Seeing these bears
do those same behaviors

00:31:01.292 --> 00:31:03.928 line:0%
that their cousins
in the wild also have.

00:31:03.928 --> 00:31:05.896 line:0%
They learned to pounce
on the ice,

00:31:05.896 --> 00:31:08.032 line:0%
and they learned to dive
in the water,

00:31:08.032 --> 00:31:11.836 line:0%
and so this is exactly
what we want for them.

00:31:11.836 --> 00:31:13.271 line:0%
They did amazing.

00:31:13.271 --> 00:31:14.972 line:0%
We've seen them since they were
these little bitty things.

00:31:14.972 --> 00:31:16.207 line:0%
I mean, they were the size
of a stick of butter,

00:31:16.207 --> 00:31:17.675 line:0%
about a pound,

00:31:17.675 --> 00:31:19.243 line:0%
they would fit in the palm of
your hand when they were born.

00:31:19.243 --> 00:31:21.245 line:0%
Now they're bears, you know,
like, we've seen them

00:31:21.245 --> 00:31:22.580 line:0%
and we've cared for them
and we've loved them,

00:31:22.580 --> 00:31:25.083 line:0%
but now they're out there
being bears.

00:31:27.585 --> 00:31:30.922 line:0%
It's been a really fun week
for all of us here at the zoo.

00:31:35.259 --> 00:31:39.731 line:0%
(music)

00:31:39.731 --> 00:31:43.368 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: 16 rescued macaws
 arrive at the Columbus Zoo.</i>

00:31:43.368 --> 00:31:45.470 line:0%
<i> If their medical exam
 checks out,</i>

00:31:45.470 --> 00:31:47.872 line:0%
<i> they will be given new homes.</i>

00:31:49.707 --> 00:31:52.309 line:0%
SUZI: Office pets? For everyone?

00:31:52.309 --> 00:31:53.744 line:0%
RANDY: Not me.

00:31:53.744 --> 00:31:57.047 line:0%
WOUTER: You're like Oprah.
Everybody gets humpback whales!

00:31:57.047 --> 00:31:57.949 line:0%
(laughs)

00:31:57.949 --> 00:31:59.683 line:0%
RANDY: Everybody
gets a macaw.

00:31:59.683 --> 00:32:00.618 line:0%
WOUTER: And a cheetah.

00:32:00.618 --> 00:32:01.685 line:0%
SUZI: Macaws for everybody.

00:32:01.685 --> 00:32:03.654 line:0%
WOUTER: Yeah.

00:32:03.654 --> 00:32:05.456 line:0%
Be careful.

00:32:05.456 --> 00:32:06.657 line:0%
That's all you.

00:32:06.657 --> 00:32:07.525 line:0%
RANDY: Not knowing
its history,

00:32:07.525 --> 00:32:08.759 line:0%
we're gonna run a whole panel

00:32:08.759 --> 00:32:11.162 line:0%
where you swab the eyes,
the mouth, and the rectum,

00:32:11.162 --> 00:32:13.831 line:0%
and then we'll send it in for
tests for infectious diseases.

00:32:13.831 --> 00:32:18.703 line:0%
We're going to assume nothing
and do a complete examination.

00:32:18.703 --> 00:32:20.070 line:0%
WOUTER: Doesn't close it much.

00:32:20.070 --> 00:32:21.539 line:0%
DAVID: This one feels
a little stiff.

00:32:21.539 --> 00:32:23.241 line:0%
RANDY: Yeah.
DAVID: It's pretty fused.

00:32:23.241 --> 00:32:24.642 line:0%
RANDY: Yeah.

00:32:24.642 --> 00:32:26.711 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Three macaws appear
 to have medical issues</i>

00:32:26.711 --> 00:32:28.112 line:0%
<i> and are weak.</i>

00:32:28.112 --> 00:32:30.915 line:0%
<i> The doctors must investigate
 right away.</i>

00:32:30.915 --> 00:32:32.950 line:0%
WOUTER: This guy definitely
is not doing great.

00:32:32.950 --> 00:32:34.284 line:0%
(squawk) Oh, shh, shh, shh.

00:32:34.284 --> 00:32:35.553 line:0%
DAVID: The issues that we were
kind of worried about:

00:32:35.553 --> 00:32:39.390 line:0%
slightly droopy wings,
not acting totally right.

00:32:39.390 --> 00:32:40.691 line:0%
RANDY: With birds,
they are very adept

00:32:40.691 --> 00:32:42.393 line:0%
at hiding clinical signs,

00:32:42.393 --> 00:32:45.362 line:0%
so when a bird looks a little
off, it's probably pretty ill.

00:32:45.362 --> 00:32:47.398 line:0%
It could, in fact, be
a life-threatening situation

00:32:47.398 --> 00:32:48.832 line:0%
with a severe infection.

00:32:48.832 --> 00:32:50.969 line:0%
RANDY: Everybody, you guys
step back a little bit.

00:32:54.338 --> 00:32:56.140 line:0%
SUZI: During those procedures,

00:32:56.140 --> 00:32:58.543 line:0%
we noticed one of the macaws
had an egg.

00:32:58.543 --> 00:32:59.944 line:0%
She should have laid it by now,

00:32:59.944 --> 00:33:02.680 line:0%
so she hasn't,
so we're very concerned.

00:33:02.680 --> 00:33:04.748 line:0%
How long would she normally
hold the egg?

00:33:04.748 --> 00:33:06.216 line:0%
RANDY: It should be laid
in five days.

00:33:06.216 --> 00:33:08.652 line:0%
SUZI: Could that be an egg
that's been there for months?

00:33:08.652 --> 00:33:10.821 line:0%
RANDY: Could be, yeah.
DAVID: Yeah.

00:33:10.821 --> 00:33:12.256 line:0%
SUZI: When a bird
hasn't laid an egg,

00:33:12.256 --> 00:33:14.859 line:0%
it can cause all sorts
of problems.

00:33:14.859 --> 00:33:18.496 line:0%
We don't know how long
this egg has been there.

00:33:18.496 --> 00:33:20.431 line:0%
We're hoping that we can
extract the egg,

00:33:20.431 --> 00:33:23.066 line:0%
but we're not sure what
we're going to get into

00:33:23.066 --> 00:33:24.535 line:0%
when we get in there.

00:33:24.535 --> 00:33:29.206 line:0%
If we don't take care of it,
it can eventually kill her.

00:33:29.206 --> 00:33:30.541 line:0%
RANDY: It's pretty clear that
it's not going anywhere

00:33:30.541 --> 00:33:32.476 line:0%
without some assistance.

00:33:32.476 --> 00:33:34.145 line:0%
(groans)

00:33:34.145 --> 00:33:35.646 line:0%
SUZI: Can you get it out whole?

00:33:35.646 --> 00:33:37.448 line:0%
RANDY: That's our intention,
that's our goal.

00:33:37.448 --> 00:33:40.050 line:0%
So let's, uh, let's go get it.

00:33:40.050 --> 00:33:42.152 line:0%
SUZI: Now do you have
to surgically do this?

00:33:42.152 --> 00:33:43.654 line:0%
RANDY: We're hoping not.
SUZI: OK.

00:33:43.654 --> 00:33:45.289 line:0%
RANDY: That's the last option.

00:33:45.289 --> 00:33:46.958 line:0%
SUZI: Whenever I'm
in the operating room,

00:33:46.958 --> 00:33:48.392 line:0%
I'm always a nervous wreck,

00:33:48.392 --> 00:33:51.129 line:0%
especially one of my animals
that I'm responsible for.

00:33:51.129 --> 00:33:53.398 line:0%
But I really feel bad
for this bird,

00:33:53.398 --> 00:33:55.733 line:0%
because she's had
such a rough life.

00:33:55.733 --> 00:33:57.200 line:0%
It just hurts me.

00:33:57.200 --> 00:33:58.002 line:0%
DAVID: Feel anything?

00:33:58.002 --> 00:33:59.036 line:0%
RANDY: Yeah, I can feel the egg.

00:33:59.036 --> 00:34:00.204 line:0%
DAVID: Good.

00:34:00.204 --> 00:34:02.272 line:0%
SUZI: So how, are you
suctioning it out or...?

00:34:02.272 --> 00:34:03.474 line:0%
RANDY: Just massage it.

00:34:03.474 --> 00:34:04.809 line:0%
SUZI: And it's moving,
you can feel it?

00:34:04.809 --> 00:34:06.377 line:0%
RANDY: It's not,
it hasn't yet.

00:34:06.377 --> 00:34:08.879 line:0%
The idea is to lube up the egg
as well as we can

00:34:08.879 --> 00:34:10.715 line:0%
and to gently massage it
down the tract.

00:34:10.715 --> 00:34:12.015 line:0%
But I can't get the egg to move,

00:34:12.015 --> 00:34:15.286 line:0%
it's lodged tightly
in the reproductive wall.

00:34:15.286 --> 00:34:17.221 line:0%
DAVID: So this situation
can be very dangerous,

00:34:17.221 --> 00:34:19.156 line:0%
just 'cause if
that egg ruptures,

00:34:19.156 --> 00:34:22.460 line:0%
all of that yolk and everything
can actually go into her abdomen

00:34:22.460 --> 00:34:27.298 line:0%
and cause some wicked infection
and generally death.

00:34:27.298 --> 00:34:29.166 line:0%
SUZI: Not moving?

00:34:29.166 --> 00:34:30.534 line:0%
RANDY: It's broken.

00:34:30.534 --> 00:34:32.904 line:0%
The eggshell is fractured
and collapsed,

00:34:32.904 --> 00:34:34.338 line:0%
so now we're just suctioning out
the contents.

00:34:34.338 --> 00:34:36.107 line:0%
We were unable to
get it out intact.

00:34:36.107 --> 00:34:37.908 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR:
 With the egg in pieces,</i>

00:34:37.908 --> 00:34:40.411 line:0%
<i> this just became an emergency.</i>

00:34:50.588 --> 00:34:52.289 line:0%
SUZI: Not moving?

00:34:52.289 --> 00:34:53.590 line:0%
RANDY: It's broken.

00:34:53.590 --> 00:34:55.259 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: A macaw's condition
 becomes serious</i>

00:34:55.259 --> 00:34:57.995 line:0%
<i> when an egg inside her breaks.</i>

00:34:57.995 --> 00:34:59.730 line:0%
DAVID: The egg is
fractured in there,

00:34:59.730 --> 00:35:04.001 line:0%
so we're gonna help gently
flush out her uterine area,

00:35:04.001 --> 00:35:06.170 line:0%
and then we're gonna suck out
all the contents of the egg,

00:35:06.170 --> 00:35:09.573 line:0%
so it doesn't travel the
opposite way into her abdomen,

00:35:09.573 --> 00:35:14.978 line:0%
and then we need to try
to tease that eggshell out.

00:35:14.978 --> 00:35:16.313 line:0%
RANDY: So you can see
the back half of the eggshell

00:35:16.313 --> 00:35:17.214 line:0%
has collapsed.

00:35:17.214 --> 00:35:18.515 line:0%
SUZI: OK, yeah.

00:35:18.515 --> 00:35:19.683 line:0%
RANDY: And now we've suctioned
all the content out.

00:35:19.683 --> 00:35:20.951 line:0%
SUZI: Could she pass that?

00:35:20.951 --> 00:35:22.386 line:0%
RANDY: Yeah, oh, yeah,
I think she'll pass that.

00:35:22.386 --> 00:35:23.888 line:0%
SUZI: OK, good.

00:35:25.823 --> 00:35:27.424 line:0%
DAVID: The egg cracked
pretty quickly,

00:35:27.424 --> 00:35:28.826 line:0%
and that indicates to me

00:35:28.826 --> 00:35:30.895 line:0%
that the egg's probably not
a healthy egg to begin with,

00:35:30.895 --> 00:35:32.964 line:0%
and this could be due
to it being in there

00:35:32.964 --> 00:35:34.731 line:0%
for a longer period of time.

00:35:34.731 --> 00:35:36.366 line:0%
Knowing what this bird
has gone through

00:35:36.366 --> 00:35:38.035 line:0%
and knowing the trauma
and neglect

00:35:38.035 --> 00:35:39.637 line:0%
that it's probably experienced,

00:35:39.637 --> 00:35:41.572 line:0%
if we can help her be
as healthy as she can be,

00:35:41.572 --> 00:35:44.108 line:0%
that's a win in my book.

00:35:44.108 --> 00:35:45.842 line:0%
SUZI: Is she coming out?
SHAWN: She's blinking.

00:35:45.842 --> 00:35:47.211 line:0%
RANDY: Awful?
WOMAN: 'My cloaca hurts.'

00:35:47.211 --> 00:35:49.046 line:0%
SUZI: Yeah, she's gonna
bite you in about...

00:35:49.046 --> 00:35:50.380 line:0%
RANDY: What? After all
I've done for her?

00:35:50.380 --> 00:35:52.550 line:0%
(laughs)
Hi.

00:35:52.550 --> 00:35:55.653 line:0%
DAVID: We're about to hear
some screaming very shortly.

00:35:55.653 --> 00:35:56.620 line:0%
(bird moans)

00:35:56.620 --> 00:35:57.955 line:0%
There she is.

00:35:57.955 --> 00:35:58.956 line:0%
SUZI: Don't kid yourself.

00:35:58.956 --> 00:36:00.458 line:0%
DAVID: OK, baby.

00:36:00.458 --> 00:36:03.461 line:0%
She's back, her brain's back on,

00:36:03.461 --> 00:36:04.929 line:0%
she's recovered well
from anesthesia,

00:36:04.929 --> 00:36:07.764 line:0%
and she hates me just as much.

00:36:07.764 --> 00:36:09.533 line:0%
SUZI: I am protective
of these birds,

00:36:09.533 --> 00:36:12.469 line:0%
because they've been
down a crappy path,

00:36:12.469 --> 00:36:16.674 line:0%
and I feel responsible
to get them out of that path

00:36:16.674 --> 00:36:20.577 line:0%
and to get them
in a perfect situation.

00:36:20.577 --> 00:36:22.213 line:0%
There's your new bird, David.

00:36:22.213 --> 00:36:24.448 line:0%
DAVID: Yeah, I'm sure
she loves me.

00:36:24.448 --> 00:36:25.483 line:0%
SUZI: Yeah.

00:36:25.483 --> 00:36:26.616 line:0%
(bird moans)

00:36:26.616 --> 00:36:28.785 line:0%
DAVID: Baby, it's OK.

00:36:28.785 --> 00:36:31.922 line:0%
(moaning)

00:36:31.922 --> 00:36:32.890 line:0%
(laughs)

00:36:32.890 --> 00:36:34.124 line:0%
PRIYA: Was that you or her?

00:36:34.124 --> 00:36:35.626 line:0%
It sounds like<i> The Exorcist.</i>

00:36:35.626 --> 00:36:38.695 line:0%
(moaning)

00:36:38.695 --> 00:36:40.731 line:0%
DAVID: Shh, shh, shh.

00:36:42.667 --> 00:36:44.335 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Out at
 the Conservation Park,</i>

00:36:44.335 --> 00:36:46.036 line:0%
<i> Flicka the horse is hopefully</i>

00:36:46.036 --> 00:36:48.406 line:0%
<i> working her way back
 into the herd.</i>

00:36:48.406 --> 00:36:49.339 line:0%
ZACK: Alright, so this morning
my big goal

00:36:49.339 --> 00:36:51.041 line:0%
is to try to find Flicka.

00:36:51.041 --> 00:36:52.175 line:0%
We brought her in

00:36:52.175 --> 00:36:53.977 line:0%
to see if there was
anything wrong with her.

00:36:53.977 --> 00:36:56.947 line:0%
She was acting a little bit off,
was staying away from the group,

00:36:56.947 --> 00:36:59.717 line:0%
and we had our veterinarians
run some blood work

00:36:59.717 --> 00:37:01.151 line:0%
and do a full analysis on them

00:37:01.151 --> 00:37:02.953 line:0%
to see if there was
anything wrong with her.

00:37:02.953 --> 00:37:05.890 line:0%
And turns out her blood work
came back positive,

00:37:05.890 --> 00:37:07.925 line:0%
and there was no issues
at all with her.

00:37:07.925 --> 00:37:10.027 line:0%
Turned out Flicka had a little
bit of cataract on her eye,

00:37:10.027 --> 00:37:11.428 line:0%
which isn't a great concern,

00:37:11.428 --> 00:37:13.530 line:0%
I mean, with her age
and where she's at,

00:37:13.530 --> 00:37:14.698 line:0%
those are things that happen.

00:37:14.698 --> 00:37:16.166 line:0%
But our biggest concern
right now

00:37:16.166 --> 00:37:19.237 line:0%
is that she's gonna incorporate
herself back in with the group.

00:37:21.906 --> 00:37:23.407 line:0%
Come on, girls.

00:37:23.407 --> 00:37:26.844 line:0%
Come on, Petra, Barbie.

00:37:26.844 --> 00:37:27.911 line:0%
Good girls.

00:37:27.911 --> 00:37:29.813 line:0%
Good morning, Barbie.

00:37:29.813 --> 00:37:31.281 line:0%
Barbie's the leader
of the group.

00:37:31.281 --> 00:37:34.518 line:0%
She wants to kick me
if I get too close.

00:37:34.518 --> 00:37:36.887 line:0%
Flicka, you feeling
any better today?

00:37:36.887 --> 00:37:39.990 line:0%
So now that we realize that
the blood work came back great

00:37:39.990 --> 00:37:42.192 line:0%
and health is not of concern,

00:37:42.192 --> 00:37:43.694 line:0%
I want to see how they interact
with each other,

00:37:43.694 --> 00:37:44.995 line:0%
so I want to keep watching her

00:37:44.995 --> 00:37:47.164 line:0%
and making sure that
there's no reason

00:37:47.164 --> 00:37:49.700 line:0%
why she's wanting to stay away
from the group.

00:37:49.700 --> 00:37:51.134 line:0%
So you can see Flicka still
keeps to the outside,

00:37:51.134 --> 00:37:52.470 line:0%
stays back a little bit,

00:37:52.470 --> 00:37:55.006 line:0%
but for the most part
she's here with the group.

00:37:58.309 --> 00:38:00.677 line:0%
Flicka, you seem to be
acting a little better.

00:38:00.677 --> 00:38:04.248 line:0%
For the most part,
she looks sound.

00:38:04.248 --> 00:38:05.883 line:0%
Whole herd's looking
really great.

00:38:05.883 --> 00:38:07.351 line:0%
No ill effects of any sort,

00:38:07.351 --> 00:38:09.987 line:0%
so really happy
everything went so well

00:38:09.987 --> 00:38:12.523 line:0%
and that everybody's
looking good.

00:38:12.523 --> 00:38:15.559 line:0%
Flicka is back with the herd,
completely incorporated herself,

00:38:15.559 --> 00:38:16.994 line:0%
and back to normal.

00:38:16.994 --> 00:38:19.563 line:0%
It's a huge sense of relief
to know that she's OK

00:38:19.563 --> 00:38:21.064 line:0%
and that the herd's
doing better.

00:38:21.064 --> 00:38:22.466 line:0%
Working with
Przewalski wild horses

00:38:22.466 --> 00:38:24.902 line:0%
is something that is a blessing.

00:38:24.902 --> 00:38:27.604 line:0%
This is an animal that is
almost extinct in the wild.

00:38:27.604 --> 00:38:29.372 line:0%
Who knows, Flicka could
have a baby one day,

00:38:29.372 --> 00:38:31.776 line:0%
and that baby could end up
back in its native range.

00:38:31.776 --> 00:38:34.578 line:0%
So it's really exciting to be
able to work with an animal

00:38:34.578 --> 00:38:38.783 line:0%
and increase their
global populations here in Ohio.

00:38:47.758 --> 00:38:50.694 line:0%
SUZI: I think the yard looks
perfect, the weather is perfect.

00:38:50.694 --> 00:38:53.396 line:0%
And there's really no way we're
gonna know if Emmett's joint

00:38:53.396 --> 00:38:55.766 line:0%
is really healed
unless he can run.

00:38:55.766 --> 00:38:58.369 line:0%
SHAWN: Yeah, definitely.
Let's see how he does.

00:38:58.369 --> 00:39:01.037 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Today, for the first
 time since surgery,</i>

00:39:01.037 --> 00:39:03.707 line:0%
<i> Emmett's shoulder will
 be put to the test.</i>

00:39:03.707 --> 00:39:05.342 line:0%
SUZI: The exciting thing
about today

00:39:05.342 --> 00:39:08.478 line:0%
is as people that come
to see Emmett running,

00:39:08.478 --> 00:39:09.646 line:0%
this is his first time,

00:39:09.646 --> 00:39:11.314 line:0%
and they're going
to have no idea

00:39:11.314 --> 00:39:12.983 line:0%
what this animal
has been through,

00:39:12.983 --> 00:39:15.952 line:0%
what the challenges have been
to get him to this point,

00:39:15.952 --> 00:39:17.455 line:0%
the surgeries, the rehab,

00:39:17.455 --> 00:39:21.458 line:0%
everything it has taken to get
Emmett to be healthy enough

00:39:21.458 --> 00:39:24.294 line:0%
that he can actually
run after the lure

00:39:24.294 --> 00:39:28.198 line:0%
and be the cheetah that
we're so excited to see.

00:39:28.198 --> 00:39:30.267 line:0%
SHAWN: Guys, we're gonna
go ahead and give this a shot.

00:39:30.267 --> 00:39:32.903 line:0%
If you want to get Emmett ready,
lure's already set.

00:39:32.903 --> 00:39:35.205 line:0%
WOMAN ON RADIO: We copy,
Shawn, that sounds good.

00:39:35.205 --> 00:39:36.840 line:0%
SUZI: If Emmett can come
out of that chute

00:39:36.840 --> 00:39:38.442 line:0%
and he can run
and chase that lure

00:39:38.442 --> 00:39:41.545 line:0%
and not be limping or
stop in the middle of it,

00:39:41.545 --> 00:39:43.214 line:0%
then we're gonna know he's OK.

00:39:43.214 --> 00:39:44.948 line:0%
This is the moment of truth.

00:39:44.948 --> 00:39:46.750 line:0%
WOMAN ON RADIO:
Alright, releasing Emmett.

00:39:46.750 --> 00:39:50.354 line:0%
SHAWN: Alright, get ready.

00:39:50.354 --> 00:39:52.857 line:0%
SUZI: Here he goes. This is it.

00:39:56.626 --> 00:39:57.728 line:0%
SUZI: This is
the moment of truth.

00:39:57.728 --> 00:39:59.229 line:0%
If Emmett can come
out of that chute

00:39:59.229 --> 00:40:01.798 line:0%
and he can run and chase
that lure and end it

00:40:01.798 --> 00:40:04.835 line:0%
and not be limping or stop
in the middle of it,

00:40:04.835 --> 00:40:07.605 line:0%
then we're gonna know he's OK.

00:40:07.605 --> 00:40:09.673 line:0%
Good job, buddy.

00:40:09.673 --> 00:40:12.476 line:0%
Oh, my gosh!

00:40:12.476 --> 00:40:13.343 line:0%
SHAWN: Look at him cheat.

00:40:13.343 --> 00:40:15.279 line:0%
SUZI: Oh.

00:40:15.279 --> 00:40:16.112 line:0%
EMILY: Good boy!

00:40:16.112 --> 00:40:17.147 line:0%
SHAWN: Oh, oh, oh, oh!

00:40:17.147 --> 00:40:18.048 line:0%
EMILY: Come on!
SUZI: Come on, buddy!

00:40:18.048 --> 00:40:19.850 line:0%
SUZI: Look at him!

00:40:19.850 --> 00:40:22.019 line:0%
Oh-ho-ho!

00:40:22.019 --> 00:40:24.321 line:0%
Good job, buddy!

00:40:24.321 --> 00:40:26.423 line:0%
You did it!

00:40:26.423 --> 00:40:29.960 line:0%
Look at him, he's a runner.

00:40:29.960 --> 00:40:32.996 line:0%
It started off a little shaky,
but that's normal.

00:40:32.996 --> 00:40:36.800 line:0%
But his legs look good,
he has very straight legs.

00:40:36.800 --> 00:40:39.770 line:0%
Cheetahs have a flat tail
that acts as a rudder,

00:40:39.770 --> 00:40:43.340 line:0%
and you could see Emmett using
that tail to make that turn,

00:40:43.340 --> 00:40:45.376 line:0%
and then once he was
in the straightaway,

00:40:45.376 --> 00:40:47.678 line:0%
man, he nailed it,
he was full-blown,

00:40:47.678 --> 00:40:50.013 line:0%
you could see him just
shooting down the runway,

00:40:50.013 --> 00:40:53.083 line:0%
all four feet up in the air
at the same time,

00:40:53.083 --> 00:40:55.152 line:0%
and that's what we want to see.

00:40:55.152 --> 00:40:57.287 line:0%
That's one beautiful cat.

00:40:57.287 --> 00:41:00.991 line:0%
You did so good.
You did so good.

00:41:00.991 --> 00:41:02.426 line:0%
SHAWN: Listen to the purr,
there's the purr.

00:41:02.426 --> 00:41:04.428 line:0%
SUZI: You're proud of yourself.
We got Emmett back!

00:41:04.428 --> 00:41:05.963 line:0%
SHAWN: I know.

00:41:05.963 --> 00:41:07.464 line:0%
SUZI: I think he's going to be
one of our fastest runners.

00:41:07.464 --> 00:41:09.466 line:0%
SHAWN: Could be.

00:41:09.466 --> 00:41:10.567 line:0%
SUZI: We did this!

00:41:10.567 --> 00:41:11.968 line:0%
SHAWN: Nice job.

00:41:11.968 --> 00:41:13.904 line:0%
SUZI: Good job, Emmett.

00:41:20.644 --> 00:41:23.647 line:0%
EMILY: You ready to go? We're
gonna go to a new room today.

00:41:23.647 --> 00:41:25.716 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: After months
 of extensive training,</i>

00:41:25.716 --> 00:41:29.920 line:0%
<i> the rescued macaws are ready to
 explore new areas of the zoo.</i>

00:41:29.920 --> 00:41:31.287 line:0%
SUZI: Today's an exciting day,

00:41:31.287 --> 00:41:33.524 line:0%
because our macaws
that we rescued

00:41:33.524 --> 00:41:35.625 line:0%
are finally coming
out of quarantine,

00:41:35.625 --> 00:41:36.894 line:0%
and it's been a long haul,

00:41:36.894 --> 00:41:38.795 line:0%
because we had a lot
of health issues,

00:41:38.795 --> 00:41:42.099 line:0%
and we were able to find homes
for 13 of the birds.

00:41:42.099 --> 00:41:45.034 line:0%
Three of them we kept back,
and that was on purpose,

00:41:45.034 --> 00:41:47.771 line:0%
because all three of those
still have medical issues.

00:41:47.771 --> 00:41:49.573 line:0%
I'm very excited
to bring them over here,

00:41:49.573 --> 00:41:51.808 line:0%
because I really don't know
how they're going to react.

00:41:51.808 --> 00:41:55.245 line:0%
It's going to be their
first time out of the hospital.

00:41:55.245 --> 00:41:56.647 line:0%
EMILY: What do you think
about this?

00:41:56.647 --> 00:41:57.815 line:0%
There we go!

00:41:57.815 --> 00:42:00.150 line:0%
SUZI: Look at her.

00:42:00.150 --> 00:42:01.585 line:0%
EMILY: You like it?

00:42:01.585 --> 00:42:03.654 line:0%
The female hyacinth came to us

00:42:03.654 --> 00:42:06.189 line:0%
not wanting anything
to do with people.

00:42:06.189 --> 00:42:09.793 line:0%
We worked really hard, and
now she is excited to see us,

00:42:09.793 --> 00:42:11.295 line:0%
she runs to the front
of her cage.

00:42:11.295 --> 00:42:14.732 line:0%
She's really opened up
and come a long, long way.

00:42:14.732 --> 00:42:16.533 line:0%
I didn't think she'd be able
to learn to step up

00:42:16.533 --> 00:42:18.034 line:0%
because of her balance,
but she did,

00:42:18.034 --> 00:42:19.769 line:0%
and she's just you know,
surprising us, so...

00:42:19.769 --> 00:42:21.171 line:0%
SUZI: She's doing great.

00:42:21.171 --> 00:42:24.908 line:0%
These birds were in not a good
situation for a long time.

00:42:24.908 --> 00:42:27.143 line:0%
And it took an army to get them

00:42:27.143 --> 00:42:29.779 line:0%
in the shape that
they're in now.

00:42:29.779 --> 00:42:34.150 line:0%
EMILY: OK, do you think you can
handle both of these birds?

00:42:34.150 --> 00:42:35.152 line:0%
SUZI: If you go get
the other one?

00:42:35.152 --> 00:42:36.119 line:0%
EMILY: Uh-huh.
SUZI: Yeah.

00:42:36.119 --> 00:42:37.421 line:0%
BRANNON: This is
strictly Grover's

00:42:37.421 --> 00:42:39.523 line:0%
'cause she gets certain pellets.

00:42:39.523 --> 00:42:41.024 line:0%
This is a lot!

00:42:41.024 --> 00:42:43.360 line:0%
SUZI: Stop. I won't feed it
all to them.

00:42:43.360 --> 00:42:45.262 line:0%
My daughter works
in the department,

00:42:45.262 --> 00:42:46.930 line:0%
and she gets really mad at me,

00:42:46.930 --> 00:42:50.167 line:0%
because they take training
so seriously, which they should.

00:42:50.167 --> 00:42:52.703 line:0%
EMILY: You guys,
you're gonna be OK.

00:42:52.703 --> 00:42:55.172 line:0%
Suzi, don't feed them
all the food.

00:42:55.172 --> 00:42:57.307 line:0%
SUZI: I'm not.

00:42:57.307 --> 00:42:58.909 line:0%
EMILY: Don't feed her
all the food, Suzi.

00:42:58.909 --> 00:43:01.278 line:0%
SUZI: I'm not spoiling her.

00:43:01.278 --> 00:43:03.980 line:0%
I don't sneak in
macadamia nuts every day.

00:43:03.980 --> 00:43:08.452 line:0%
Come on, it's fun to spoil
people and animals.

00:43:08.452 --> 00:43:10.454 line:0%
OK, I better stop.

00:43:10.454 --> 00:43:12.923 line:0%
They have no idea, do they?

00:43:12.923 --> 00:43:15.192 line:0%
(squawk)

00:43:15.192 --> 00:43:16.426 line:0%
EMILY: Hey, Nige.
SUZI: Plenty of food here!

00:43:16.426 --> 00:43:18.028 line:0%
EMILY: How you doing?

00:43:18.028 --> 00:43:20.531 line:0%
We brought your girlfriend.

00:43:20.531 --> 00:43:24.034 line:0%
We have the two scarlet pair,
Nigel and Adele.

00:43:24.034 --> 00:43:25.769 line:0%
Getting them out of the woods

00:43:25.769 --> 00:43:28.104 line:0%
and taking care of all
their medical issues,

00:43:28.104 --> 00:43:29.472 line:0%
it took a lot of time.

00:43:29.472 --> 00:43:31.375 line:0%
Every single day,
multiple times a day,

00:43:31.375 --> 00:43:33.610 line:0%
working with them,
gaining their trust.

00:43:33.610 --> 00:43:35.278 line:0%
SUZI: Hi, you guys.

00:43:35.278 --> 00:43:36.746 line:0%
You're out of quarantine.

00:43:36.746 --> 00:43:38.048 line:0%
EMILY: What do you think?

00:43:38.048 --> 00:43:40.717 line:0%
SUZI: It took a while,
this one was so scared and shy.

00:43:40.717 --> 00:43:41.852 line:0%
EMILY: Yeah.

00:43:41.852 --> 00:43:43.219 line:0%
I think they're ready
to go places.

00:43:43.219 --> 00:43:44.854 line:0%
SUZI: I think I would definitely
start using them,

00:43:44.854 --> 00:43:46.290 line:0%
I would take them out.

00:43:46.290 --> 00:43:50.360 line:0%
I think it's gonna be good for
them to get in new environments,

00:43:50.360 --> 00:43:52.562 line:0%
and they look great.

00:43:52.562 --> 00:43:54.431 line:0%
She just even looks happier,
you know?

00:43:54.431 --> 00:43:56.366 line:0%
EMILY: Mm-hmm.

00:43:56.366 --> 00:43:59.703 line:0%
Suzi Rapp, I see you.
I have great peripheral vision.

00:43:59.703 --> 00:44:02.272 line:0%
SUZI: I was just looking
for an almond.

00:44:02.272 --> 00:44:03.974 line:0%
EMILY: After this,
we're going to be fat birds.

00:44:03.974 --> 00:44:05.742 line:0%
SUZI: It's pretty pathetic
that I'm the boss,

00:44:05.742 --> 00:44:07.511 line:0%
and I'm the one
who gets in trouble.

00:44:07.511 --> 00:44:08.845 line:0%
BIRD: Hello.

00:44:08.845 --> 00:44:11.281 line:0%
(laughs)

